<?php
/** User manual — Staff edition: what every Golden A/S employee does for himself in the portal — leave, program, training, evaluations, community.   GAS-CP-R63-003 */
return [
  'title' => 'Customer Portal — Employee Guide',
  'lede'  => 'Your own corner of the portal: your leave balance and requests, the leave periods you can join, your program on the Planning board, the trainings you can apply for and your certificates, your annual evaluation, your messages with HR, and the Community — wishes, kudos, the noticeboard and badges. Short, with the steps to follow.',
  'sections' => [
    ['id' => 'start', 'title' => 'Getting in and getting around', 'body' => [
      'Filters — type to pick from the list (v1.195.3): a drop-down became a type-to-search list only when it had more than eight entries, so a short filter such as Vessel on the purchase orders opened as a plain list. Now, in every filter bar of the portal, a drop-down with four or more entries opens with a search box: click it, type a few letters, press Enter or click the entry. The Vessel and Supplier filters of Purchase orders and of the Pipeline board are always searchable, whatever their length. Fixed choices of two or three entries (for example Purpose) stay plain lists.',
      'QHSE documents for everybody, and approved suppliers in purchase orders (v1.164.0): QHSE › Documents (library) shows the approved documents of the quality, environmental, health & safety and business continuity systems — manual, policies, procedures, work instructions, forms — to read and download; the valid version is the one shown there. For procurement: the supplier list of a new purchase order is grouped — Approved suppliers first, then Not evaluated yet, then Not approved; a supplier that the quality department marked Not approved cannot receive a purchase order (requests for quotation are not limited); Procurement › Suppliers shows the approval of each supplier, and Settings › Procurement › Approved suppliers can limit purchase orders to approved suppliers only and gives the Approved supplier list (Excel).',
      'QHSE has its own handbook (v1.163.0): Support › QHSE Handbook describes the new menu group QHSE — the registers of ISO 9001, ISO 14001, ISO 45001 and ISO 22301, the calibration of measuring equipment and the validity of its certificates, and who has access. A staff role named QHSE, HSE or Quality has no access to the financial data of the company: amounts are hidden and the financial pages are closed for it.',
      'Leavers — warnings (v1.169.1): Settings › Users lists at the top the accounts still open for people who are not active in HR; the daily health report counts them. See the HR handbook, People register.',
      'Purchase order flow — paid in advance on a proforma (v1.195.2): an order paid in full against the supplier\'s proforma showed the steps Invoiced and Paid in yellow, which read as "not paid". Yellow meant "in progress": the payment was made on a proforma and the supplier\'s sales invoice has not been assigned yet. Now, when the proforma covers the whole order and is paid in full, the step Paid is green with the note "100 % · paid in advance". The step Invoiced stays yellow with the note "proforma — sales invoice awaited" until Accounting assigns the sales invoice on the proforma (Payables › the proforma › "Assign the invoice"); then it turns green too. The steps On the way and Received are grey until the goods or services are received on the order.',
      'Production — amount of a subcontract entered by the engineer (v1.195.1): the field Amount of a subcontracting line was shown only to users whose position sees financial data, so a mechanical engineer who creates the subcontract could not record the amount agreed with the subcontractor. Now whoever may edit the production order — production users, the manager of the order and the project manager — has the fields Amount and currency in "Subcontracting" on the order page and sees the amounts of the lines of that order. Nothing else opens: the Subcontracting list across all orders, its total and the PDFs still show amounts only to positions that see financial data, and a user marked "no financial data" (QHSE) never sees or enters them.',
      'BOM and requisition — BOM price or last buying price (v1.195.0): until now the column Unit price of a BOM file was not read for the Engineering BOM, so the estimate of a line always came from the buying history of the inventory item ("Last buying price"). Now the Unit price of the file is kept on every line as its BOM price, and above the lines of the BOM and of the requisition there is the choice "Estimated prices from: Last buying price / BOM price". One click recalculates all lines and the total; the origin under each price says which one is used, and with "Last buying price" selected the BOM price is shown under it for comparison. A line without a BOM price keeps the buying price; an item never bought takes the BOM price in both cases. For a BOM or requisition imported before this version, use "Take the BOM prices from the file" next to the choice and upload the same Excel again: the prices are matched to the lines by description, else by maker part number or code, nothing else on the lines changes, and the estimate switches to BOM price. A requisition made from a BOM starts with the choice of the BOM, and a purchase order made from them takes the BOM price when that is the choice (otherwise the price rule of the supplier as before). The choice can be changed by Procurement, by the engineer of the BOM and by the requester while the requisition is a draft; every change is written in the log.',
      'Purchase orders — vessel shown and filter by vessel (v1.194.0): in Purchasing › Purchase orders the column Project now shows, under the project and the sub-project, the vessel of the project (⚓ name), so it is clear for which vessel an order is. The same line appears on the cards of the Pipeline board, on the requests for quotation, on the lines "To buy" and on the header of the order page. The filter bar has a new field Vessel (also on the Pipeline board): pick a vessel to see only its orders; clicking the vessel name in a row does the same. The search box also finds orders by vessel name. The vessel comes from the project card — an order without a project (stock, own use) has no vessel.',
      'Payables — invoice, order, project and sub-project stay together (v1.193.2): an invoice or proforma registered from a purchase order always took the project and the sub-project of the order, but the first time its header was saved again (a changed amount, date or note) the portal dropped the order and with it the project — the page then showed "No purchase order — checked by hand", the cost left the sub-project and attached files had no project to go to. This is corrected: the order of a saved invoice is kept on every edit. The project and the sub-project come from the order; when the order itself has none, they are taken from the requisition its lines came from. An invoice without an order now has the fields Project and Sub-project on its header. On an invoice that is already checked, requested or paid, Accounting finds "Purchase order, project and sub-project" under the header and links it afterwards with "Save the link" — only orders of the same supplier are offered, and the cost moves to the right sub-project. A payment request lists the order and the project › sub-project of each invoice, with "no project — link it" where one is missing. Invoices that lost their order before this version get it back automatically when their lines still point to the order; any other is linked by hand as above.',
      'Payables — files of an invoice without a project (v1.193.1): the message "The payment is recorded, but the file was not kept" appeared whenever a proof of payment was attached to an invoice that belongs to no project (stock, IT, overheads): the portal files documents in the project, and with no project it had no place for the file — the payment itself was always recorded correctly. Such files are now kept with the invoice: the PDF of the invoice or proforma, the invoice assigned to a proforma, and the proof of each payment. They open with the 📎 beside the invoice number and on the line of the payment, are read only by those who have Payables, and the proofs are ticked as attachments of the remittance e-mail like the others. A payment recorded without its proof — because of this fault, or because the bank document came later — has "Attach the proof" on its line. Allowed: PDF, JPG, PNG, WEBP, Excel, Word up to 15 MB; a file whose content is not what its name says is refused, and then the message tells you so. Invoices of a project are filed in the project as before.',
      'Purchase orders — the pipeline (v1.193.0): Procurement › Purchase orders has a new tab, "Pipeline": a board with seven columns, one per stage an order can be waiting in — Draft (to be sent), Sent (waiting for the confirmation), Confirmed (on order / on the way), Partly received (rest still to come), Received (invoice to come), Invoiced (to be paid), Paid (to be closed). Every order that is not closed or cancelled stands in exactly one column: the first step of its flow that is not finished. Each column shows how many orders it holds and, for those who see prices, their total. A card shows the number of the order (click to open it), the supplier, the project and title, the expected date and the flow in small; a red edge and a red mark mean "delivery late" or "payment overdue", a gold edge "no confirmation for 7 days", and "paid in advance" marks an order paid before the goods arrived. The same three counts stand at the top of the board. Filter by supplier or search as in the list. The board is read from the orders as they are — nothing is dragged; an order moves by itself when it is sent, confirmed, received, invoiced and paid.',
      'Purchase orders — where each order stands (v1.192.0): every purchase order now carries a flow of eight steps, drawn as circles joined by a line: Created → Sent to the supplier → Confirmed by the supplier → On the way → Received → Invoiced → Paid → Closed. Green with a tick is done, gold is in progress, a grey number is not yet, red with "!" needs attention. On the order the flow stands under the title with the detail of each step: the dates, the supplier\'s reference, the last logistics milestone and the expected date (red when the delivery is late), the share received and the date of the last receipt, the share invoiced (or "proforma of the supplier"), the share paid with the due date (red when overdue; "paid in advance" when a proforma was paid before the invoice). In Procurement › Purchase orders the column "Progress" shows the same flow in small for every order — hover a circle to read the step. It is read from what is already recorded (sending, confirmation, logistics, receipts, payables); nothing is typed for it. It shows percentages only, never amounts, so everybody who can open the order can follow it. An RFQ has its own short flow: Created → Sent → Quotation received → Awarded.',
      'Production orders — new numbers (v1.192.0): production orders are now numbered GAS-PRD-2026-001 (GIT-PRD-… for the Italian branch) and no longer GAS-PO-…, which was the code of the purchase orders. The existing production orders were renumbered by the update — the same year and running number, only "PO" became "PRD" — and new ones continue the sequence. Production cards, serial numbers and the links from sub-projects follow by themselves. Papers printed before the change still carry the old number: the running number is the same.',
      'Process flow — from the offer to the delivery (v1.191.0): Support has a new card, "Process flow". It is one portrait page, read from top to bottom, for Sales and Purchasing: twelve steps in five groups — Sales (System Budget, offer, offer approved by the customer), Engineering (bill of materials), Purchasing (requisition, request for quotation, purchase order), Warehouse (receipt of goods, issue to the project, delivery note) and Accounting (supplier invoice, proforma and payment of the customer). In the middle stands the step and who does it — a click opens its page. On the left, in green, what the step does to quantities and to the stock, with the marks "into stock", "reserved" and "out of stock"; on the right, in gold, what it does to money. Below are two lists: everything that changes the stock (receipt, reservation at approval, issue and delivery note, parts on a service report, manual movement and transfer, adjustment) and how delivery notes work (four kinds, made from the inventory or from what was received, booked as the issue of a requisition or as an exit, cancelling brings the quantities back, no prices on the PDF). "Print" gives the page on paper for training. The page shows the process only — no figures of the company.',
      'Purchase order — the estimated prices of the requisition (v1.190.2): when a requisition line becomes a line of an order, its price is, in this order: the supplier\'s price for the item, the last buying price, the price on the item card — and, when none of these is known, now the estimated price typed on the requisition, marked "estimate of the requisition <number>". Before, the estimate was carried only for lines without an inventory item, so an item never bought before arrived without a price. For orders made earlier, a draft order shows "Take the estimated prices of the requisition (n lines)" under the table: it fills only the lines that have no price and whose requisition line has an estimate. An estimate is a starting figure for the total of the order, not the supplier\'s price — replace it in the table, or with "Prices from the supplier\'s offer", before the order is sent.',
      'Purchase order — "Still to buy in this project, from other requisitions" (v1.190.1): the box at the top right of the lines of a draft order, until now "Pull requisition line", is renamed. It is not part of the conversion of a requisition into an order — the lines of the order are already there. It lists the approved requisition lines of the same project (or sub-project) that still have a quantity to buy and are not on this order; choose one and press "Add this line to the order" only if you want to buy it from this supplier on this order. The box is not shown when nothing is left to buy.',
      'Purchase order — lines edited in the table (v1.190.0): on an order or RFQ that can still be edited, every line of the table is its own form: description, note, quantity, unit price, discount %, lead time and needed-by date are typed where they stand, and "Save the lines" under the table saves all of them at once — only the lines that changed are written, the totals are recalculated and the history records how many lines changed. A comma or a dot works for decimals (1.234,50 is read as 1234.50); an empty price clears it; a quantity cannot go below what was already received. A price you type is marked "typed"; a price left as it was keeps its origin (last buying price, supplier price list …). The pencil ✎ and the form below remain only for changing the inventory item of a line or adding a line.',
      'Purchase order — prices from the supplier\'s offer, read by the assistant (v1.190.0): under the lines, "Prices from the supplier\'s offer": choose the offer or quotation the supplier sent — PDF, Excel (.xlsx) or a photo / scan, up to 8 MB — and press "Read the offer". The assistant looks for each line of the order in the document, by the maker part number first, then our code, then the description, and puts price, discount and lead time in the fields of the table, marked in gold, with what it found and how certain it is ("offer · 95 % · …"). Nothing is saved: check the gold fields against the offer — those under 70 % first — correct what is wrong and press "Save the lines" (on a sent RFQ: "Save quotation"); "Discard the proposals" drops them. A price saved as proposed is marked "supplier offer … — read by the assistant, checked", a corrected one "typed"; the supplier\'s offer number is written to the order if it has none. The banner warns when the offer is in another currency (prices are not converted) and repeats validity or minimum-order remarks. To switch it on: Settings › AI assistant › tick "Purchase orders — prices from the supplier\'s offer", and the user needs the assistant on his or her account. The file and the lines of the order (code, maker, part number, description, quantity) are sent to the assistant; the file is not kept in the portal — attach it to the order yourself if you want it filed.',
      'Maker part number from the file, and all new items in one step (v1.189.2): (1) A BOM created with its Excel attached read the columns with an older list of names — "Maker part no" and "Golden code" of our own template were not on it, so the lines came in without the part number, nothing could be matched to the inventory and no code could be proposed. The column names are now the same in every import, and a row is matched to the inventory by our code or by the maker part number. (2) Lines already in an order, requisition or BOM without their numbers: the card "Lines without an inventory item — similar items" has "Take the numbers from the file" — choose the file the lines came from; each line takes maker part number, maker and code from the row with the same description, and where that part number is already in the inventory the line is linked to the item at once. (3) The Code field of "New inventory item" then holds our code for the part: the prefix of the maker as used on that maker\'s items, a dot and the maker part number (SE.18639 for Schneider Electric 18639) — or the code of the file when it has one. For a maker with no item in the inventory yet there is no prefix to take, so type the first code and the next ones follow. (4) "Create the n new items with the proposed codes" makes them all in one step; lines whose part number is the same or nearly the same as an existing item are left out for you to decide with "Use".',
      'The code of the imported file on a new inventory item (v1.189.1): when a BOM file is imported into a purchase order, an RFQ or a requisition, a row whose code is not in the inventory becomes a free-text line — and its code was dropped, so "New inventory item" under "Lines without an inventory item — similar items" stood empty. The code of the file now stays on the line: it is shown under the description ("code in the file") and stands in the Code field, so ＋ creates the item with it (you can still change it; if the field is emptied the code of the file is used before the maker part number). It also travels with the line from the BOM to the requisition and to the order. For lines imported before this version, the card has "Take the codes from the file": choose the same file again and its codes are put on the lines with the same description — nothing else of the line changes and no line is added. First check the similar items: "Use" links the line to an item that already exists instead of making a second code for it.',
      'Where each person stands, and equipment prepared by departments (v1.189.0): HR now sees a process flow of onboarding (card, account, job description, equipment, first weeks) and of handover at leaving on every employee card and on its dashboard. IT, QHSE and the warehouse prepare the equipment of their own categories under HR › Equipment to give and HR issues the list you accept under My equipment. Safety equipment and anything with a life carries a "Replace by" date — you are notified 30 days before. Procurement: a new supplier is sent to QHSE for its initial evaluation by itself.',
      'My equipment (v1.188.0): what the company gives you to work with — instruments, tools, laptop, telephone, working clothes, safety equipment, keys and cards — is recorded by HR and listed under HR › My equipment. When HR sends you an Equipment issue list you get a notification: check the lines, tick the box and press Accept; if something does not agree (a wrong size, a missing item, a different serial number) write it in "Report a difference" instead and HR corrects the list. When you give something back, tick it and press "Hand over the ticked lines", then give it to HR, who accepts the list line by line; at the end of the employment everything in your name is handed over this way. Every list has a PDF. The equipment remains the property of the company: report loss, theft or damage at once.',
      'Portal usage — session duration (v1.187.1): Settings › Login history now has the card "Portal usage — sessions and their duration". Every sign-in of a member of staff opens a session; its duration runs from the sign-in to the last activity (a page opened or a form sent — a page left open without work adds no time), and it ends with the sign-out, the idle time-out, the closing of the browser or a new sign-in. For the last 7, 30, 90 or 365 days the card shows, per person: sessions, days with a sign-in, total time, time per session and the last sign-in, with the totals on top; a click on a name lists that person\'s sessions with start, last activity, duration, how it ended and the browser. Only administrators see it; customers are not measured. Recording starts with this version — earlier sign-ins have no duration. For the staff: the time you are signed in to the portal is recorded as described here.',
      'People who left (v1.187.1): a person whose employment has ended in HR is out of the celebrations, the lists and the notifications, and can no longer come back by an Excel update or a card save; the portal checks it every day. What such a person still held — a discipline, a team, running projects, open tasks — is listed for HR to hand over (HR handbook).',
      'The HR year (v1.187.0): one calendar for everybody — targets in January, training plan in February, target reviews in April, July and October, summer leave groups in July, targets closed and evaluations drafted in November, HR review and company closures in December, your acknowledgement of the final evaluation by 31 January. You find it at the bottom of My leave with the current month marked. HR follows the same calendar on its dashboard with what is done, due and late, and is warned of delays by notification and a weekly e-mail (HR handbook).',
      'Job descriptions (v1.186.0): HR › Job descriptions holds what the company expects from each position of a discipline. HR writes them (the company\'s own fourteen are ready to load as drafts, and the assistant can draft a new one), and each is approved in order by the Discipline Manager, the General Manager and the CEO — you are notified when one waits for you and approve or send it back on its page. Every employee has "My job description" in the HR menu: read it, press "I have read it" and take it as PDF if you wish; you are asked again when a new revision is approved. Vacancies take their texts from the approved job description of the discipline. The full description is in the HR handbook.',
      'Quality — no financial data, and the Milestones and Safety log (v1.185.2): the financial data of the company was closed only for a staff role named QHSE, HSE or Quality, while the QHSE menu was also given to positions named QA or QC — so a "QA/QC" position could work in QHSE and still see amounts. Now one rule decides both: an account is Quality when its staff role or its discipline is named QHSE, HSE, EHS, Quality, QA or QC (also in Greek), or when the person is a member of the QHSE team. For such an account amounts are hidden everywhere and the financial pages are closed: pipeline, offers, system budgets, sales, collections, cash flow, cost control, payables, invoices and proformas, and the prices of the inventory. An administrator or a member of Management who is named in the QHSE team keeps the figures. Quality now has the page "Milestones & safety log" in the QHSE menu: it lists the running projects with their milestones, the ones not yet achieved and the last safety entry; open a project to add, change or remove milestones and safety entries (lost-time incidents, near misses, observations) — the same page project managers use, which feeds the weekly and monthly reports. To check a person: Administration › Users shows the staff role and discipline; QHSE › Overview shows the team.',
      'Signed out on the screen when the idle time runs out (v1.185.1): the portal has always ended your session after the idle time (30 minutes unless your administrator set another value), but a page left open stayed on the screen and you only saw the sign-in page at your next click — so it looked as if you were still signed in, and whatever was on the page stayed readable. Now the open page itself goes to the sign-in page with the message "You were signed out after a period of inactivity" as soon as the time is up, and also when you come back to the tab. Work in another tab of the portal counts as activity for all tabs. Save your form before leaving the desk: an unsaved form on an expired page is lost.',
      'Payables — proforma to prepay, invoice assigned later (v1.185.0): when a supplier asks for payment in advance, register his proforma instead of waiting for the invoice: Accounting › Payables › "Register proforma (prepayment)", or choose "Proforma — to be paid in advance" in the field Document of a new supplier invoice ("Sales invoice of the supplier" is the other choice, as before). A proforma linked to a purchase order takes the ordered lines — the goods are not in yet, so it is checked against what was ordered, not what was received. From there it is the same road as an invoice: Check, payment request, approval, payment, payment e-mail. It carries the mark "Proforma" and the list has the filter "Proforma waiting for the invoice". When the supplier sends the sales invoice, open the proforma and fill the card "Assign the supplier\'s sales invoice": number, date and the PDF. The invoice takes the place of the proforma on the same document — the proforma number is kept and shown, amounts, payments and the request stay, nothing is registered twice. If the invoice differs in amount from the proforma, register the difference as a separate invoice or credit note.',
      'Payables — supplier statement as PDF, Excel and e-mail (v1.185.0): Accounting › Payables › Supplier statement: choose the supplier and the dates; "PDF" opens the statement as a document with our letterhead (date, kind, reference, order, due date, debit, credit, running balance, totals and the balance due), "Excel" downloads the same as a workbook, and ✉ E-mail under the table prepares the message to the supplier with the PDF attached (tick the Excel too if he wants it). The Excel button used to fill the screen with unreadable characters instead of downloading — corrected.',
      'Inventory — items left out of the valuation (v1.185.0): on the item card (Edit) tick "Do not include this item in the inventory valuation". The item keeps its stock, movements, prices and orders; only its value is left out of the totals: the Valuation tab (value at last price, at average cost and per category), the value shown above the item list and the Value column of the Excel. The Valuation tab says how many items are left out and what their stock is worth, with a link to list them; such an item carries the mark "not in valuation". Only Procurement administrators can change it.',
      'Payables — payment proof attached to the e-mail (v1.184.3): when you record a payment on a supplier invoice and add the bank confirmation in "Payment document", that file is now attached by itself to the e-mail to the supplier: open ✉ E-mail on the paid invoice and the proofs of all its payments are listed under Attachments, already ticked — untick one you do not want to send. Before, the e-mail had no attachment and the file had to be added by hand. One limit: the portal keeps a file only for an invoice that belongs to a project; for an invoice without project (stock or own use) the form says so, and if you still choose a file a red message tells you it was not kept — attach the bank confirmation to that e-mail by hand.',
      'Price proposed on a purchase order line (v1.184.2): pick an inventory item on a purchase order and the Unit price field is filled at once with the price the portal proposes, with a line under it saying where it comes from ("Proposed: Last buying price · date · supplier", or the rule set in Inventory › Settings). Before, the field stayed empty and the price was put in only when the line was saved, so it looked as if nothing was proposed. Change the figure if the supplier quoted another price — the line is then marked "typed"; leave it and the line keeps the origin. An item that has a last price on its card but no line in its price history (older imports) was not proposed at all: it now is, marked "Last price on the item card"; the same holds for a BOM imported into an order and for the estimate of a requisition line. Nothing is proposed on a request for quotation (the supplier gives the price) and for an item that never had a price. Prices are shown only to those who see buying prices.',
      'Existing inventory code not found on a purchase order (v1.184.1): on a purchase order the box "Inventory item" searched only among the items of the supplier of that order (items bought from him, preferred from him, or of a maker he represents), so an item of the inventory that had never been bought from that supplier did not appear and only "New inventory item" was offered. Now the supplier only orders the list: an exact code or maker part number comes first, the supplier\'s items next, then the rest of the inventory. Typing the code (or the maker part number) in full and leaving the box picks the item by itself — description, maker and unit fill in — on purchase orders, requisitions and Engineering BOMs. In the BOM import, a row is also linked when our own code was written in the part-number column, or the maker part number in the code column. If the portal still offers a new item for a code you know exists, the item is marked Obsolete or Blocked in the inventory, or the code differs by a character: open Inventory and search there.',
      'Work permits — several employees under one permit (v1.184.0): the permit form now has the list "Employees covered by this permit" with a tick box for each active employee of the HR registry and a filter by name above it; tick everybody the permit is for (a yard pass or a port pass for a team, for example) and save. The register lists the names under the permit, the search finds a permit by the name of any of its people, and the expiry warning names them. Holder is now the title of the permit or a free name: leave it empty and it takes the names of the ticked employees; for a subcontractor or agency person who is not in the HR registry, type the name there and tick nobody. Permits made earlier keep their employee, already ticked. Who may edit is unchanged: administrators, HR, Accounting and the project manager of the project.',
      'Work permits — Accounting (v1.183.2): people marked Accounting (Settings › Users) can now add and update work permits of every project, like HR and the administrators. A project manager still edits the permits of his own projects; everybody else in the company sees the register without the edit buttons.',
      'VAT on a purchase order (v1.183.1): the VAT % is set in the header of a draft order (Subtotal, VAT and Total then appear on the page and on the order document). Until now the rate could not be changed once the order was sent, because the header is read-only from then on — an order sent with 0 % stayed without VAT. Now the header of a sent, confirmed or received order shows "VAT %" with a Set button for Procurement: type the rate (0 to 30) and press Set; the totals follow and the change is written in the order log. The PDF and the e-mail attachment are also worked out again from the lines and the rate each time they are opened. A closed or cancelled order is not changed.',
      'Code of a new item = prefix.maker part number (v1.183.0): our inventory codes are the maker prefix, a dot and the maker part number (SE.A9F74332). The portal now proposes that code wherever a new item can be made. BOM import review: under "New inventory item" each unmatched line shows "Code for a new item: SE.A9F74332" and where the prefix comes from — the prefix used on the items of that maker, or, when the maker is not filled in the inventory, the prefix of the similar items. Procurement sees the code in a field and can change it before Apply; engineers see it as information. Card "Lines without an inventory item" (requisition, purchase order, BOM): the same hint under each line and the proposed code already in the New inventory item field. When the code already exists the hint says so — use that item instead. When no prefix is known for the maker the field stays empty and shows prefix.<part number>: type the code; an item is never created with the bare part number. The more items carry their maker name, the more often the prefix is found by maker.',
      'Budget pricing requests — processed ones hidden (v1.182.1): Procurement › Budget pricing requests opens on the tab "Waiting for prices" and shows only the budgets that still need prices. The budgets already priced are under the tab "Priced"; "All" shows both. Each tab carries its count.',
      'Received per project and vessel (v1.182.0): Procurement › Inventory › Delivery notes › "Received per project" (also from the Procurement tab of a sub-project). The page lists, by vessel, project and sub-project, every line the warehouse holds or expects for the project: lines of approved requisitions — marked "bought for the project" when they came in on a purchase order, or "from stock, reserved" when stock covered them — and lines of project purchase orders made without a requisition. Columns: Needed, In warehouse (ready to send), Sent, To come. Filter by vessel or project; Show: ready to send, ready and still to come, or everything. The storekeeper ticks lines (or the box of a whole sub-project), changes the quantity to send when only part goes, fills date, attention, address, carrier and reference, chooses one note per sub-project or one per project, and presses Issue delivery note(s). The stock leaves the warehouse, requisition lines are marked as issued and the notes appear under Delivery notes. A quantity above what is in the warehouse for the project is refused. Cancelling a note brings the goods back and reserves them again for the same requisition, so they show as ready once more. Viewing: procurement and warehouse staff; issuing: warehouse.',
      'One path to purchase orders (v1.181.0): whichever way you start, the steps are now the same — lines (typed, from a BOM workbook, or from an approved BOM) → inventory proposals for free-text lines → draft purchase orders per supplier → check prices and send. (1) Purpose of an order: a new purchase order or RFQ asks for its purpose — Project (the project is then required and the order appears in the sub-project\'s Procurement tab), Stock (replenishment of the warehouse, no project) or Own use (consumables, tools and equipment for ourselves, no project). The Purchase orders list has a Purpose filter and marks Stock and Own use orders. Existing orders without a project were marked Stock. (2) Requisition: a draft requisition has "Import BOM (Excel) into this requisition" (same template, up to 400 rows); the card with similar inventory items follows. (3) From requisition to orders: on an approved requisition Procurement sees "Purchase orders of this requisition" — what is still to buy by supplier (preferred supplier of the item, else the last one, else the supplier of the maker) and the button Create draft purchase orders; lines without a known supplier go to the supplier you choose. This is the same step as "Purchase orders from this BOM"; the To buy basket remains for mixing lines of several requisitions. (4) The Procurement tab of a sub-project lists its RFQs together with its purchase orders.',
      'Choosing inventory items everywhere (v1.180.0): (1) Wherever a line is free text — a requisition, a purchase order or RFQ, a BOM (also lines typed by hand) — the card "Lines without an inventory item — similar items" under the lines lists up to four inventory items per line with their stock; Use links the line to the item (quantity unchanged; the typed text goes to the note; a typed maker or part number stays if the item has none). The System Budget BOM review shows the same proposals under each row: Use writes the item code into "other item" and ticks Accept. (2) A part number that is our code without its maker prefix (A9N15656 ↔ SE.A9N15656) is now matched automatically at import, when only one item has it. (3) New inventory codes are created by Procurement only. Engineers leave the line as free text — in the BOM import review "New inventory item" is available to Procurement only. Procurement sees a column "New inventory item" in the same card: type the code (or leave it empty to use the maker part number) and press ＋; the item is created from the line and linked. A code that exists is refused. (4) Procurement › Inventory › Import › "Maker part numbers from our codes": per code prefix, the number of items without a maker part number and how often, for the items that have one, it equals the code after the dot. Tick only prefixes that are makers (those at 80 % or more are pre-ticked) and press Fill; only empty fields are written, and the last fill can be undone.',
      'Similar items instead of new codes (v1.179.0): (1) BOM import review (sub-project › Procurement › BOM › Import): under every line that was not matched exactly the portal now lists up to four similar inventory items — same part number inside our code (for example A9N15656 and SE.A9N15656), similar code or maker part number, or similar description — each with its stock (in stock / free / no stock) and a Use button. Use chooses that item for the line and ticks Accept. Next to "Create new item" the mark "similar items exist" reminds you to look first. (2) Purchase orders and RFQs: a draft order has "Import BOM (Excel) into this order" (same template as the Engineering BOM, up to 400 rows per file). Rows whose Golden code or maker part number is in the inventory become lines linked to the item; the others become free-text lines. Below the lines, "Lines without an inventory item — similar items" proposes inventory items with stock for every free-text line, also for lines typed by hand; Use links the line (code, unit, maker and part number of the item; the typed text is kept in the note; quantity unchanged). Nothing is created in the inventory by the import. (3) Delete a BOM: 🗑 Delete on the BOM page asks for the reason; administrators, management and Procurement may delete a revision in any status, unless a requisition / kit or purchase-order lines made from it still exist — delete those first. The deleted revision is listed under Requisitions › Deleted documents.',
      'Cancel and delete in Procurement (v1.178.0): (1) Cancel on a purchase order asked for the reason and then did nothing — corrected; the same fault affected the other buttons that ask for a typed reason (supplier invoice, delivery note, RFQ supplier). (2) Delete: a purchase order or RFQ shows 🗑 Delete while it is a Draft or Cancelled; a requisition while it is a Draft, Rejected or Cancelled. An order that was sent must be cancelled first — that returns its requisition lines to "to buy". You type the reason; the document, its lines and its history are removed and the sub-project cost line of the order with them. Delete is refused, with the reason shown next to the button, when something depends on the document: goods receipts, supplier invoices, delivery notes, stock movements or reservations, logistic costs, an order awarded from the RFQ, or (requisition) lines that are on an order. Who: procurement administrators delete drafts; a cancelled order only management or a portal administrator; the requester an own draft requisition. (3) Deleted documents: at the bottom of the Purchase orders and Requisitions lists, management and procurement administrators see number, title, supplier or requester, reason, who and when. The numbers are not used again.',
      'Projects list — faster (v1.177.1): the list of projects showed the same pills and percentages but calculated budget, engineering hours and procurement progress for every sub-project, also for those without a budget, requisition or order. These calculations now run only where such data exists; what you see is unchanged.',
      'System budget — up to 1024 part lines (v1.177.0): a System Budget now holds up to 1024 part lines (before: 124) and "Import BOM parts (Excel)" reads up to 1024 lines per file (before: 400). Working with a long list: change what you need anywhere in the table and press Save lines once at the bottom — all lines travel together and only the changed ones are written. Lines beyond 1024 are refused with a message; split the system into two budgets. The assistant reads the first 250 part lines of a budget and matches 125 imported BOM lines per press — press again for the next ones. When such a budget is converted to an offer, choose "parts as one line" unless the customer needs every part listed. If a page was open before the update and Save answers that the form has too many fields, reload it with Ctrl+F5 and save again — nothing was lost or half saved.',
      'System budget — parts at procurement (v1.176.1): after Send to procurement the part lines are locked until procurement returns the prices. The Parts card now shows this with a notice and the button Take back; the fields are read-only and Save lines, the delete ticks, the BOM import and Add from inventory are hidden meanwhile. To delete or change a part line: press Take back (in the Parts card or in the Procurement card), tick the 🗑 box of the lines, press Save lines, and send the budget to procurement again if prices are still needed. Also new on every page: when a save is refused, the page goes to the red message instead of staying at the section you were working in.',
      'Inventory import (v1.176.0): Procurement › Inventory › Import now has the button Import template (.xlsx) — an empty workbook with the columns Code, Description, Description EN, Category, Unit, Qty, Last price, Location, Maker, Factory code, Barcode, Min stock, Supplier, one example line and a Read me sheet. The ERP warehouse export can be uploaded as it comes, with its title line above the header: the portal looks for the header in the first 15 lines and tells you on which line it found it. Always press Preview first and check the four numbers (lines read, new, existing, with a price) before Apply; a line whose code exists updates the item, stock is written only for lines with a quantity, and the same file can be imported again without doubling anything. One file may hold up to 30 000 lines; a large file takes a few minutes — wait for the green message and do not press Apply twice.',
      'Owner\'s copy — monthly report and vessel history (v1.175.0): the monthly sub-project report (sub-project › Reports) and the vessel technical history (vessel page) work as the weekly report since v1.174.0. For staff the gold buttons give the Owner\'s copy — exactly what the customer sees, without internal notes, internal comments, internal open issues, drafts and timesheet hours — and the plain buttons the Internal version, named …-INT. When filing, choose Owner\'s copy — shared with the customer (default) or Internal copy — staff only (number with -INT, listed under Internal copies filed).',
      'Reports filed earlier (v1.175.0): Settings › System health lists, for administrators and management, the weekly, monthly and vessel-history revisions that were filed for the customer before Owner\'s copies existed. They were built under a staff account and may contain internal content. Open a file to check it; Make internal takes that revision away from the customer while staff keep it; Make all internal does it for the whole list. File the report again as Owner\'s copy if the customer should have it.',
      'Weekly report — Owner\'s copy (v1.174.0): the weekly vessel report on screen is the internal version for staff — it includes internal notes, internal ticket comments, internal open issues and our drafts. What goes to the customer is the Owner\'s copy, built exactly as the owner sees the report. Download: the gold buttons Owner\'s copy PDF / Word; the plain buttons Internal PDF / Word give the internal version, whose file name ends in -INT. E-mail: the attachments list offers both and the Owner\'s copy PDF is the one ticked — tick an Internal file only for a colleague. File: next to File as revision choose Owner\'s copy — shared with the customer (the default; it appears in the customer\'s Documents) or Internal copy — staff only (filed under the same number with -INT and never shown to the customer; listed under Internal copies filed).',
      'Risk log in the weekly report (v1.173.0): every risk is internal and stays in the Risk log of the project. On the risk form tick Show in the weekly report to share it — the log marks it In weekly report. The weekly vessel report then prints, under Potential delays foreseen, the table Risk log with the marked risks that are open, accepted or occurred and those mitigated or closed in the week: number, title with the mitigation, sub-project, score, owner, due date and status, a mark on the risks raised in the week, and one line with the counts (open, high, new, mitigated). Risks that are not ticked never appear in the report, in the PDF, the Word file or the copy the owner opens. The owner\'s executive summary counts only the shared risks, and the assistant that drafts the summary text receives only those.',
      'BOM to purchase orders (v1.172.0): an approved BOM of a sub-project has the card Purchase orders of this BOM. For Procurement it lists what is still to buy — quantity minus free stock, minus what is already on an order — split by supplier: the preferred supplier of the item, else the one it was last bought from, else the supplier of the maker; for lines without a known supplier choose one or leave them for later. Create draft purchase orders raises one draft per supplier, linked to the project, the sub-project and the BOM lines, with the proposed prices. The drafts stand in Procurement › Purchase orders, where they are checked, sent to the supplier and followed to receipt; the card lists them with their status. Pressing again never orders a line twice. A supplier that is Not approved gets no order and is named. When the BOM already has a requisition, the orders are raised from the requisition instead (Procurement › To buy). Once an order is sent, the Procurement stage of the sub-project follows it.',
      'Process — Procurement fed from the procurement process (v1.171.0): on the Process card of a sub-project the stage Procurement fills itself from the requisitions and purchase orders of the sub-project, as Engineering does from its sections and Production from its orders. A requisition line counts 50 % when it is covered — ordered from a supplier or reserved from stock — and 100 % when it is in the warehouse or issued; lines of purchase orders raised without a requisition count the same way. Lines weigh by their value; when a value is missing every line weighs the same. A requisition waiting for approval counts 0 %; drafts, rejected and cancelled ones do not count. The field is then read-only and says 🛒 fed from the procurement process with the number of lines, requisitions and direct orders, linked to the Procurement tab; Unlock gives the value back to the project manager, Lock returns it to the feed. A sub-project without requisitions and purchase orders keeps the value typed by the project manager and the N/A tick.',
      'Production orders — cyber safety mark (v1.170.0): New / Edit production order has the tick Cyber safety approval (IACS UR E26 / E27). Tick it when the equipment of the order is a computer-based system that has to comply; its serial numbers are then offered in IT & Cybersecurity › Marine cyber safety for the UR E27 documentation and the team is told. The order shows the mark UR E26 / E27. Orders without the tick are not shown there.',
      'Marine cyber safety speaks of Equipment (v1.169.0): its records are the equipment we produce, linked to the serial number of the Equipment register; the page of a serial number shows its cyber safety card. People who are not active in HR no longer stand in the lists of personnel. See the IT & Cybersecurity and HR handbooks.',
      'IT & Cybersecurity has its own handbook (v1.162.1): Support › IT & Cybersecurity Handbook describes remote support contracts, reporting a security event, the ISO 27001 registers with supplier evaluations and training, marine cyber safety (IACS UR E26 / E27) and the purchases of the IT department. Changes to that menu group are described there from now on.',
      'Speed — fewer database statements per page (v1.158.0): every page used to ask the database some forty times whether a table or a column exists; these questions are now answered from a copy of the table and column list that the portal keeps for five minutes. Nothing changes in what you see; pages need fewer round trips to the database. In System health › Slow statements the line “SHOW TABLES LIKE …” should stop growing; press Clear there to start a fresh count. A table or column added by an update is seen at once.',
      'Service reports — Older documents (v1.157.2): the list “Older documents” at the bottom of the tab Field service reports is shown again; in v1.157.1 it was missing. Nothing else changed on the page.',
      'Service reports — three tabs, filters and what needs action (v1.157.1): the page Service reports now has a tab for each kind of report: Field service reports, Inspection reports, Function checks / commissioning. Each tab shows how many reports it holds and, with a flag ⚑, how many need action. The filter bar works on the open tab: Search (report number, project, sub-project, vessel, engineer, subject), Vessel, Project, Status, Show (All / Needs action) and Order; the choice is kept when you move through the pages, Clear removes it. Needs action: under the status of a report the next step is written — Finish and submit (draft), Get the customer’s acceptance — sign on device or send the link (awaiting acceptance), Sent back — correct and submit again, Process the external report, and for inspection reports Finish and submit / Check and issue. The yellow line above the list says how many reports of the tab need action; “Show only these” lists them. A customer sees “Your acceptance is needed” on the reports waiting for their acceptance.',
      'Doubled sub-projects are merged by management (v1.157.0): when the same sub-project exists twice in a project, management merges the two records on Projects › Doubled codes. Afterwards there is one sub-project with all offers, files, timesheets and reports of both; nothing you entered is lost. Tell your project manager when you notice a sub-project that shows different content depending on where you open it from.',
      'Sub-project codes compared loosely (v1.156.4): the portal now treats a sub-project code as the same when it differs only in capitals, in spaces or in a Greek letter that looks like a Latin one — Υ.028 (Greek Υ), y.028 and “Y. 028” are all Y.028. New sub-project and Edit sub-project refuse such a code when another sub-project of the project has it, and name that sub-project. The import from Excel updates the existing sub-project instead of creating a second one. The page Projects › Doubled codes finds such pairs and marks the code that has a Greek letter or an unusual character — correct that code in Edit sub-project. Y.28 and Y.028 remain different codes.',
      'Service reports — filter and order (v1.156.3): the page Service reports has a bar with Vessel (type to search) and Order: Newest first, Oldest first, Vessels A → Z, Vessels Z → A. Apply filters all three lists of the page (field service reports, inspection reports, commissioning forms) and the choice is kept when you move through the pages of a list; Clear removes it. With “Grouped by vessel” on, the groups follow the vessel name (Z → A when chosen). Clicking a column header now also works in a grouped list — here and in every list of the portal grouped by vessel: the rows are sorted inside each vessel and the vessels follow their first row, so a click on Vessel orders the vessels A → Z, a second click Z → A, a click on Date brings the vessel with the newest or oldest report first. A header click sorts the rows of the page you see; for the whole list use Order.',
      'Same sub-project, different content (v1.156.2): when a sub-project shows an offer or progress when opened from Projects but not when opened from Vessels, there are two records with the same code in the project. Tell the project manager or management: they have the list Projects › Doubled codes and decide which record is kept.',
      'Payables — logistic costs of another provider on the supplier invoice (v1.156.1): the pull adds by itself only the logistic costs whose Provider is empty or is the supplier. A cost entered on the order with the carrier as Provider (DHL, DSV …) is listed on the invoice under the lines, in “Logistic costs of the order not yet on an invoice”, with the button “Add to this invoice”. Press it when the supplier charges that freight on this invoice; leave it when the carrier sends its own invoice, and register that invoice in Payables for the carrier. A cost in another currency than the invoice cannot be added.',
      'Payables — logistic costs on the supplier invoice (v1.156.0): “Pull received PO lines and logistic costs” on a supplier invoice (and the tick “Pull …” when the invoice is registered) now also adds the logistic costs entered on the purchase order — freight, insurance, customs, duties, import VAT, handling — one line each, marked “logistics”, so the payment request covers parts and logistics together. Rules: a cost is pulled when its Provider is empty or is the supplier of the invoice; a cost of another provider (a forwarder, a customs broker) is not pulled — the message names the provider, and its invoice is registered in Payables as an invoice of that provider; a cost in another currency than the invoice is not pulled; a cost goes on one invoice only. On the purchase order each logistic cost shows the supplier invoice that carries it, and it cannot be deleted while it is on an invoice. Logistic lines are not part of the three-way check. Sub-project costs: a logistic cost counts once — as Logistics when it is entered on the order; the invoice adds only the parts.',
      'Payables — “Pull received PO lines” brings nothing (v1.155.7): the button pulls only what was received and is not yet invoiced: for each line of the purchase order, the quantity accepted at goods receipt minus the quantity already invoiced. While no goods receipt is recorded on the order it pulls nothing. The message now says why: of the lines of the order, how many are already on this invoice, how many have no goods receipt, how many are fully invoiced. Record the goods receipt on the purchase order and pull again — or, when the supplier invoices before delivery, add the lines by hand with their PO line and check the invoice with “accept the difference” and a reason.',
      'Purchase order — total with logistics (v1.155.6): a purchase order now shows what it costs to pay in full. The green tile “Total with logistics” at the top is the gross of the parts plus the logistic costs entered on the order (freight, insurance, customs, duties, import VAT, handling); under it the two parts are shown. The same figure is the last row of the Logistic costs table (“Total to pay”), and the list of purchase orders shows “+ logistics · total” under the net of an order that has logistic costs. The logistic costs are added on the order under Logistic costs; they are internal and are not printed on the order sent to the supplier. Paying: the supplier invoice is registered in Payables against the order lines — when the supplier charges freight on the same invoice, add it there as an extra line without a PO line; an invoice of a forwarder or customs broker is registered in Payables as an invoice of that supplier.',
      'Purchase order and requisition — Category by typing (v1.155.5): when you add a line with a new inventory item, the Category drop-down now opens with a search box like the other long lists of the portal: type part of the name and press Enter. A list with eight entries or fewer stays a plain drop-down.',
      'Payables — what “Quantity mismatch” means (v1.155.4): the three-way check does not compare the invoice with the ordered quantity but with what was received and not yet invoiced: PO open qty = quantity accepted at goods receipt − quantity already invoiced. An invoice that agrees with the order still shows Quantity mismatch while the goods receipt of the purchase order is not recorded (PO open qty 0). Under the label each line now shows “ordered · received · invoiced before” and the reason: the goods receipt is missing (with a link to the purchase order — record the receipt there, then press Check invoice again), the quantity was already invoiced on another invoice, or the invoice has more than was ordered and received. When the supplier invoices before delivery, Accounting can press Check invoice with “accept the difference” and a reason.',
      'Payables — why an invoice cannot be ticked (v1.155.3): on Payables › Supplier invoices only invoices with status Checked have a tick box for a payment request. A Draft or Disputed invoice now shows the button “Check first” in that place. It opens the invoice: enter its lines (pull them from the purchase order, or add them — a Draft with gross 0,00 has no lines yet) and press Check invoice. The portal compares the invoice with the purchase order and the goods received; when they agree the invoice becomes Checked and can be ticked. When quantity or price differ it becomes Disputed until it is corrected or checked with a reason.',
      'Sub-project with a doubled code (v1.155.2): when two sub-projects of a project have the same code (for example after an ERP import), each of them can now be edited and saved — status, order, invoicing — as long as you leave the code as it is. Before, the message “That sub-project code already exists in this project” stopped every save. Edit sub-project shows a red note under Code naming the other sub-project; give one of the two another code when you can. Changing a code to one that is already used, or creating a sub-project with a used code, is still refused, and the message now names the sub-project that has it.',
      'BOM template (v1.155.1): one Excel template serves both BOM uploads. Download it with “BOM template” at the top of System Budgets, with “Import template” next to Parts › Import BOM parts inside a budget, with “BOM template” on the Procurement tab of a sub-project (also inside New BOM), or on a draft BOM revision. Sheet BOM has the columns Section, Qty, Unit, Description, Maker, Maker part no, Golden code, Unit price, Note; replace the two example lines with your own and keep the header row. Description is mandatory, Qty defaults to 1, Unit must be one of the sheet Units. Golden code links the line to the inventory at once; otherwise Maker and Maker part no are used to find the item. Unit price (EUR per unit, without VAT) is optional: a System Budget takes it as the cost of the line, the BOM of a sub-project ignores it. After the upload nothing is written before you review the proposed matches and press Apply. A System Budget reads at most 400 lines per file.',
      'Assessments (v1.155.0): when HR starts an assessment that includes you, you receive a notification and My leave shows the card Assessments with the button Answer. The questionnaire is in your portal language. Choose one answer per question; “Save and continue later” keeps your answers, “Submit” is final and cannot be changed. Answer as you actually work — some statements are repeated in other words on purpose. After the last day your result appears in the same card: a score 0–100 per quality, from your answers and from the rating of the head of your department, with a short guidance. Only you, the HR Manager and the CEO can read it. The scores are also listed in your annual evaluation of the same year.',
      'Service Engineer app 0.7.0 — customer acceptance (v1.154.0): the app updates by itself the next time it is opened with a connection; no new installation. Open a submitted service report under Reports. At the bottom there is now “Customer acceptance” (it needs a connection to the portal). “Customer signs on this phone”: the app hides its menu and shows the customer the report — particulars, hours, every task with parts, open issues and time lines, and the PDF — then the full name, the capacity, a remark, the tick “I have read the report”, the box to sign with a finger and the buttons “Accept and sign” / “Do not accept” (a remark is then required). After the signature the phone shows “Signed — thank you” and asks to be handed back; the report is accepted in the portal at once, with “Signed on site” in its customer box. “Send for acceptance by e-mail”: type the customer’s address (the contacts of the customer are suggested) and a name; the customer gets the personal link with the 6-digit code, valid for 24 hours. Under the buttons the app lists every link with its state — sent, opened, code confirmed, answered, cancelled, expired — and, when something went wrong, a red note with the reason and what to do (e-mail not sent, returned by the recipient’s mail server, closed after wrong codes, no answer), with “Send again” and “Cancel link”. Signing on site while you are still writing the report, without a connection: choose “on site” under Signature as before; the new button “Show the report to the customer” opens the same customer view of your draft, and “Back to signing” returns to the signature box. The portal pages for the same functions are unchanged.',
      'Acceptance by e-mail — problems are shown (v1.152.1): when something goes wrong with a link, the card “Acceptance by e-mail” says so and tells you what to do. A red line at the top of the card counts the links that need attention; the row of the link carries a warning next to its state, the reason, and the action. The cases: “E-mail not sent” — Microsoft 365 did not take the message (the link, the 6-digit code, a reminder or the confirmation), with its reason; press “Send again” or phone the customer. “Returned by the recipient’s mail server” — the message came back, usually because the address does not exist, the mailbox is full or the server refused it; the text of the return is shown; check the address with the customer and send to the right one. The portal looks for returned messages in the inbox of its own mailbox every ten minutes, for links of the last three days. “Closed” — too many wrong codes were typed; send a new link. “No answer” — the link ran out; send again or phone. A confirmation that could not be sent after the customer answered is shown in amber: the answer is recorded, send the signed document yourself. “Send again” on the row sends a new link to the same address in one click (the old link stops working). In every case you also get a notification, with a link to the document. If the first message cannot be sent at all, nothing is created and the reason is shown at once.',
      'Acceptance by e-mail (v1.152.0): for a customer who does not use the portal. Where: on a submitted service report or function check, on an offer that waits for the customer, and on an owner approval (drawings and other documents) that waits for a decision — the card “Acceptance by e-mail”. Type the recipient’s e-mail address (the contacts of the customer are suggested) and, if you wish, the name, then “Send for acceptance”. The document must have its file (the PDF of the report, the attached PDF of the offer or drawing). What the recipient gets: an e-mail from the portal’s mailbox, with your address for replies, the PDF attached and one personal link. The link opens a page without any login. First the recipient presses “Send me the code” and types the 6-digit code that arrives at the same address — this proves that the person controls that mailbox. Then the page shows the document (summary, tasks and hours for a report; subject, amount and validity for an offer; title and revision for a drawing) with the full file, and the recipient enters the name and capacity and decides: a report is accepted with a signature drawn by finger, or not accepted with a remark; an offer is approved or rejected with the reason; a drawing is approved, approved with comments, or rejected. What you see: the card lists every link with its state (sent, opened, code confirmed, answered, cancelled, expired) and “Cancel link”. When the recipient answers, the document changes status at once, the history shows “by e-mail link”, the name, the capacity and the address, you get a notification and an e-mail, and the recipient gets a confirmation — with the signed report attached. Validity: a service report 24 hours; an offer until its validity date, otherwise 14 days; everything else 14 days. Reminders go out by themselves — a service report once after 8 hours, other documents after 3 and 7 days — and each reminder carries a new link that replaces the earlier one. A link stops working when it is used, cancelled, expired, replaced by a newer link to the same address, after too many wrong codes, or when the document is decided another way (in the portal, in the app, on your device). Sending the same document to the same address again replaces the earlier link. Customers with a portal account can still accept in the portal or the app as before.',
      'Customer signs on your device (v1.151.0): when the customer is next to you and has no portal account at hand, let them sign on your phone or tablet. Submit the service report (or the function check) as usual, then press “Customer signs on this device” on the document. The portal opens a signing view without the menu, so the customer sees only the document: a summary (customer, vessel, project, date and place, who submitted it), every task with its description, parts, open issues and time lines, the hours in total, and a button that opens the full PDF. Hand the device over. The customer enters the full name and, if they wish, the capacity (Captain, Chief Engineer, Owner’s representative …) and a remark, ticks “I have read the document”, signs with a finger in the box and presses “Accept and sign” — or “Do not accept” with a remark that says what must change. The page then shows “Signed — thank you” with the signed PDF and asks for the device back. The document becomes Accepted (or goes back as Not accepted) at once; its customer box reads “Signed on site” with the drawn signature, the name, the capacity, the date and time and the network address, and the history records who signed and on whose device. The button is shown to staff while the document is submitted and waits for the customer; an accepted document cannot be signed again. Signature boxes (all service reports and function checks): the name, the capacity and the date are printed on separate lines and always stay inside the box — long texts are reduced in size and, in the extreme, shortened with “…”. Signing in the customer’s own portal account or in the customer app works as before.',
      'Dashboard speed and the health report (v1.150.1): the block “KPI cockpit” on the dashboard needs about eleven database statements for every active sub-project, which made the dashboard take two to three seconds whenever the block had to be worked out again. From this version the dashboard never waits for it: it shows the figures it has kept, and the portal renews them in the background every ten minutes while people are working. The figures of the block can therefore be up to ten minutes old (after a quiet night, the first page shows the last kept figures and they are renewed within a minute); the KPI page itself always calculates live. Staff limited to one branch have their own kept figures. Daily health report and Diagnostics: the line “Aborted connections” is now “Refused connections (whole database server)” and shows the total since the server restarted and the number per hour. The counter belongs to the whole shared database server — every account on it — so it is a warning only above 300 per hour; it concerns the portal only when the PHP error log shows connection errors at the same time. A line under “Other servers” such as “News feed · HTTP 403” means that one news site refuses automatic readers; the other feeds are not affected and the feed can be switched off in Marine intelligence › News sources.',
      'Sales pipeline — branch filter (v1.150.0): Commercial › Pipeline has a third filter, “Branch”, next to the sales person and the vessel (shown when more than one branch is active). Choose a branch and press Apply: the board, the list, the totals at the top (open offers, weighted value, overdue follow-ups), the follow-ups due, the table per sales person and the funnel of the year all show that branch only. The branch of an offer is the branch of its sub-project, otherwise of its project, otherwise the head office — the same rule as on the Offers list. Each card shows the branch code after the customer. The filter stays when you switch between board and list or change the funnel year; “Clear” removes it.',
      'KYMA mark by role (v1.149.0): contacts and organisations that have an e-mail address and are customers, yards, owners or engineering offices now get the brand mark “KYMA Marine Power” next to “Golden A/S” by themselves, so that KYMA issues can reach them. Who qualifies — organisations: linked to a customer, or with the role Customer, Yard, Owner or Consultant / designer (the engineering offices), and with an e-mail on the organisation or on one of its contacts. Contacts: with an e-mail, and either a customer contact, or with the category Owner, Owner representative, Yard, Yard manager, Engineering / technical office or Designer / naval architect, or belonging to an organisation that qualifies. The portal checks once an hour, so new and imported contacts are marked without anyone doing anything. Only the mark is added: the Golden mark, the subscriptions and both unsubscribe lists stay as they are — somebody who unsubscribed from KYMA still receives nothing. Settings › Website › “KYMA website” › “KYMA mark by role” (administrator) shows how many are waiting, has the switch, and a button “Save and mark now”. While the rule is on, a KYMA mark that is removed by hand from such a contact comes back at the next check; switch the rule off if you want to manage the marks by hand.',
      'Sub-projects — automatic order (v1.148.0): the field “Order” of a sub-project is now numbered by the portal, in the same way as the code. New sub-project: the field shows the next number (the last position) and you can leave it as it is. To put the new sub-project somewhere else, type the position you want — for example 2 — and the sub-projects from that position on move down by one. Existing sub-project: change the number to move it; the others are renumbered by themselves. The order of a project is always 1, 2, 3 … up to the number of its sub-projects, with no gaps and no two sub-projects on the same number, so you never have to look up which numbers are free. Deleting a sub-project closes the gap. The Excel import follows the same rule: a row with an order is placed at that position, a row without one goes to the end. When this version was installed, every project was renumbered once in the order it already showed (numbered sub-projects first, the rest by code), so nothing moved on screen.',
      'Organisations — known shipyards (v1.147.0): Contacts › Organisations has a button “Known shipyards” (administrators). The page lists the organisations that do not have the Yard tick yet and whose name contains either a general shipyard word in any language (shipyard, shipbuilding, dockyard, dry dock, boatyard, werft, scheepswerf, cantiere, chantiers, astillero, tersane, ναυπηγείο, stocznia and others) or the name of a known yard in Europe, the US and worldwide (for example Feadship, Lürssen, Damen, Benetti, Fincantieri, Navantia, Neorion, Sedef, Westport, Hyundai Heavy, Gulf Craft). Each row shows the present roles, city and country, the number of contacts and the word or name that matched. Opening the page changes nothing. Rows are ticked for you, except names that look like a group company which is not itself a yard (services, components, trading …) — those start unticked. Untick what is not a yard, then press “Add the Yard tick to the ticked organisations”. Only the Yard role is added: customer, supplier and the other roles stay, and the tick can be removed again on the organisation. After that, the contacts of these organisations are reached by the “Yard” choice of a newsletter audience or list, in any country. Run the page again after an import of contacts — it shows only what is new. Role “Other”: an organisation keeps “Other” only when no other role is ticked — when you save an organisation with another role, “Other” is removed by itself, and existing organisations that carried “Other” next to another role have been corrected.',
      'Newsletters — approval before sending (v1.146.0): an issue reaches the readers only after the CEO or the General Manager has approved it. Author (sales, administrator): when the issue is ready, open the card “Approval before sending” at the bottom of the issue, add a note if you wish and press “Submit for approval”. The issue shows “Waiting for approval” and is locked — nothing can be changed while it waits; “Take back” unlocks it. The CEO and the General Manager get a notification with a link. Approver: open the issue, look at the preview and the recipients, then “Approve”, “Approve and send” or “Return for changes” (a comment is required when you return it). The author is notified of the answer. Once approved, the author — or an administrator — presses “Send”. Any change to the text, the subject, the language or the audience after the approval cancels it and the issue must be submitted again, so what is sent is exactly what was approved. Issues written by the CEO or the General Manager need no approval and are sent directly. The issue and the list show the state (waiting, approved, returned) and who approved, when and for how many recipients. The rule applies to Golden A/S and KYMA issues alike. Newsletters › Settings has the switch “Approval by the CEO or the General Manager before an issue is sent”; only the CEO and the General Manager can change it. A test to your own address needs no approval.',
      'KYMA website in the portal (v1.145.0): the portal now serves two websites, Golden A/S and KYMA Marine Power, and keeps them apart. Enquiries: Website › Enquiries has a tab per site with its own counter; KYMA enquiries carry a reference KYM-year-number and the notification starts with “KYMA ·”. A topic filter narrows the list. On an enquiry, “Create or link contact” makes the sender a contact marked KYMA (the role is proposed from the topic: partnership → agent / partner, sales → customer) and, if ticked, creates the organisation; an existing contact with the same e-mail is linked instead and gets the KYMA mark added. Career applications are shown only to administrators and management, never become contacts, and an administrator can delete old ones per site (“Retention”). Statistics: Website › Statistics shows one card per site (visitors, page views, enquiries, trend, connection state) and the full breakdown below for the site you choose; each site has its own feed address and key, and a failure of one does not stop the other. Newsletters: every issue belongs to one site, chosen when it is created. A KYMA issue is sent from the KYMA mailbox with KYMA branding and KYMA privacy link, and reaches only confirmed KYMA subscribers and contacts marked KYMA, minus the KYMA unsubscribe list; a Golden issue reaches only Golden subscribers and contacts marked Golden. Unsubscribing from one site does not touch the other. Subscribers and the unsubscribe list show the site and can be filtered; a list can be limited to one brand. Contacts and organisations: a “Brands” field (Golden A/S, KYMA Marine Power, or both), a brand filter on both lists, the mark as a pill, and a Brands column in the exports. Settings (administrator): Settings › Website › “KYMA website” — generate the KYMA site key (shown once; put it in the GitHub secret of the KYMA website, never in an e-mail or chat), the KYMA statistics feed address and key; Newsletters › Settings — the KYMA sender mailbox. Until the KYMA mailbox is set, the KYMA sign-up form answers that the newsletter is not open yet, so nothing is ever sent to KYMA readers from the Golden address.',
      'System Budgets — procurement step (v1.144.0): a budget with parts now passes through procurement before it is finished. Engineer: when the part list is ready, open the card “Procurement: prices and delivery times” on the budget, add a note if needed and press “Send to procurement”. The budget shows “At procurement”, the part lines are locked and the procurement accounts get a notification; “Take back” returns it to you at any time. Procurement: Procurement › “Budget pricing requests” lists the budgets waiting, urgent and soonest first. “Fill prices” opens the part lines only — no labour, no sale prices, no margins — with the inventory item and its last purchase price beside each line. Enter per line the cost price, the delivery time in weeks, the supplier, a remark and, when there is one, an alternative part with its price and delivery. “Save” keeps your work; “Save and return to the engineer” confirms the remaining prices and sends the budget back with your note. Engineer again: the card lists what procurement gave; “Use alternative” swaps a part for its alternative; a line whose cost you change afterwards is marked. Finish is possible only when every part line with a quantity has a procurement price — lines added later need “Send to procurement again”. Management can switch this rule off on System Budgets › Consistency overview › Limits. On Convert to offer the longest delivery time is written into the offer’s “Estimated delivery time for parts”.',
      'System Budgets — consistency check (v1.143.0): every budget now shows the card “Consistency check” under its title, with ten numbered checks. 1 Arithmetic — stored totals that differ from the lines (button “Recalculate totals”), negative numbers, hours without a rate, a cost rate of zero. 2 Completeness — lines without hours, section or discipline, parts without cost or description, a code used twice, an empty description. 3 Rates and prices — each labour rate against the rate list, each inventory part against the inventory price (5 % tolerance) and prices older than 6 months. 4 Profit rules — every line, section and group against the company minimum; a line sold below cost. 5 Internal ratios — project management share, commissioning, engineering, travel and allowance outside Attica, testing, documentation, logistics. 6 Similar budgets — five key ratios against the finished budgets of the same template or of all (needs five). 7 Revision to revision — a table of every line added, removed or changed since the previous revision, with the effect on cost, sale and profit. 8 Budget to offer — the offer made from the budget still totals the same. 9 Budget to actuals — hours and costs booked on the sub-project against the budget, with the forecast. 10 AI review — the button “AI review” compares the description with the lines. Each finding is “Must fix”, “Check” or “Note”. A “Must fix” finding (wrong totals, a line below cost, impossible numbers) stops Finish and Convert to offer; management can accept it for the current revision with a written reason, which is kept in the history. System Budgets › “Consistency overview” lists all open, finished and recently converted budgets with their counts, worst first; management sets the limits (tolerances, months, percentages) at the bottom of that page.',
      'Who may export contacts and customers (v1.142.1): the Excel exports of Contacts, Organisations, Customers and Accounts are now reserved for three staff roles — CEO, General Manager and Developer. Being a portal administrator is no longer enough: for everybody else the Export button is not shown and the download address answers “reserved for the CEO, the General Manager and the developer”. The role is the Golden A/S role of the account (Settings › Accounts › the person › Golden A/S role); to let a developer export, create the role “Developer” in Settings › Roles and give it to that account. Every export is recorded with time, person, list and number of rows, and so is every refused attempt; both appear in the daily health report. Importing, viewing and editing are unchanged.',
      'System health (v1.142.0): a new page for administrators, Settings › System › System health, with six tabs. Trend — the typical page time and the slow 5 % per day for 30 days, as a chart and a table; click a page in “Pages, last 7 days” for its own trend; a step upwards on one day points at the release of that day. Slow pages — every page over 2 seconds with a bar showing where the time went: database, other servers, or the portal’s own work, and the split start / data / draw. Other servers — each call to Microsoft 365, the website, the news feeds, Softone and the AI assistant in the last 24 hours: how many, how many failed, average and slowest. Background jobs — each job the portal runs by itself with its last work, duration, result and last error. Slow statements — database statements over 100 ms grouped and ordered by total time; “Examine” shows how the database reads the statement and says when a table is read in full (a missing index). Errors — the PHP log grouped by message with count, first and last time, page and version; “New” marks what appeared after the current version went live, “Quiet” what has not come back since. The figures build up from this version on; no personal data is kept in them. The daily health report now also lists yesterday’s speed against the days before, new errors, failing calls and failing jobs.',
      'Daily health report by e-mail (v1.141.0): every day the portal runs its diagnostics and e-mails the result to the administrators. Subject line says at once whether all is OK. Content: summary (checks, failures, warnings), what needs attention with the proposed fix, the slow pages of the last 24 hours (page, how often, slowest, average), the PHP log of the last 24 hours grouped by message with a count, background work (last sweep, newsletter queue, sign-ins) and the full diagnostics. Set it in Settings › About / Versions › Diagnostics › “Daily health report by e-mail”: on / off, time (07:00 by default, company time) and who receives it — administrators only; nobody ticked means all administrators. “Send now” sends one immediately. The portal has no clock of its own: the report leaves with the first activity on the portal at or after the chosen time, so on a day when nobody opens the portal before 09:00 it arrives at 09:00.',
      'Faster pages (v1.140.4): the portal does background work — new notifications, task and permit reminders, the website statistics pull, the marine news feeds and newsletter batches. It was meant to run once a minute but, because of a time-zone slip, it ran on every page and the page waited for it, sometimes for other servers. Now it runs once a minute and only after the page is on screen, so nothing waits for it; a notification can therefore appear up to a minute later than before. Diagnostics › Slowest pages should show far fewer entries from now on — use “Clear the slow-pages log” once to start a clean list. Also fixed: opening a recorded payment for editing on a proforma or invoice (pencil in Payments) now fills the form with the payment instead of an empty one.',
      'Newsletter lists — either or both (v1.140.3): a list with both organisation types and person categories ticked used to keep only the people who fitted both (e.g. only the shipyard managers of yards), which left many lists empty. Now the default is “Either”: everybody working for a ticked kind of organisation plus everybody with a ticked category, whatever their organisation. The new field “Organisation type and person category” on the list form switches a list to “Both” when the narrow selection is wanted; the list table shows (either) or (both). Existing lists follow “Either” — open a list and choose “Both” to get the old result. Country: the field proposes the countries written on the organisations and takes several, separated by commas (Greece, Cyprus); it limits the list to people whose organisation is in that country, so a contact without an organisation is left out when a country is set.',
      'Contacts subscribed to all four lists (v1.140.2): every active contact with a valid e-mail address now has Newsletter, Campaigns, Events and Greetings ticked. The portal did this once by itself after the update; people who unsubscribed or whose address bounced were left out and stay out. A new contact starts with the four ticks (untick what does not apply before saving), and a contact created by the Excel import gets them when the Subscriptions cell is empty. On Web Sales › Newsletters › Lists the button “Subscribe n contact(s) with an e-mail to all lists” (management) applies the rule again to contacts that miss a tick — it appears only when there is something to do. A contact without an e-mail or with a malformed one is not touched.',
      'Importing contacts without overwriting (v1.140.1): Settings › Import from Excel › Contacts has a new tick, on by default — "For contacts that already exist: fill only the empty fields". A person found by e-mail (or by name and company) keeps name, job title, phones and category as they are; only empty fields are filled, the category only when it is still "Other", and the organisation only when the person has none. The report counts created, updated and "already complete (no change)". Untick it to let filled cells of the file overwrite, as before. A new optional column Country fills the country of the organisation when it has none. Always run with "Check only" first; new organisations are created only with "Create the new organisations" ticked (kind Other — set their roles under Companies afterwards, so the newsletter lists by role find them). Categories: three more — Engineering / technical office, Service / after-sales, Director / executive — and job titles in English, Italian, Dutch, Spanish and Greek are recognised more widely (electrical and automation engineers count as Engineering, not Design).',
      'Newsletter lists and contact categories (v1.140.0): (1) Lists — Web Sales › Newsletters › Lists. A list is a saved selection of contacts: tick organisation roles (customer, yard, owner, management company, supplier …) and / or person categories (captain, chief engineer, ETO, owner\'s representative, technical manager, purchasing manager, designer, shipyard manager, project manager …). Role and category narrow each other — "Yard" + "Project manager" = the project managers of yards; leave one empty to take everybody of the other. Optional: only people subscribed to the newsletter or the events, a country, addresses to always include and addresses to never include. A list is always up to date: a contact that gets one of its categories joins it by itself; the page shows the members. In a newsletter tick one or more lists under Audience (the role ticks and extra addresses remain available); each address receives one message, and people who unsubscribed are left out as always. (2) Contact categories — the Category of a person (Contacts) has more values: yacht manager, technical manager / superintendent, engineer / electrician, shipyard manager, project manager, designer / naval architect, purchasing manager, surveyor, sales, broker, agent. People still on "Other" whose job title is clear got their category automatically once after the update; later, a contact saved with category Other and a clear job title takes the category of the title, and Lists has a button to apply it again to imported contacts. (3) Website subscribers become contacts — when a person confirms the subscription, the contact with the same e-mail gets the Newsletter / Events subscription; if there is none, a new contact is created with the category of the role chosen on the website form, marked "from website / to review" and linked to an organisation only when the company name matches an existing one. Open it from the Website subscribers tab, complete organisation and category and save — saving clears the mark.',
      'Newsletters (v1.139.0): Commercial › Web Sales has a fourth tab, ✉ Newsletters (administrators, management and sales prepare; management or an administrator sends). (1) New newsletter: type a title and Create. (2) Subject: internal title, e-mail subject, language of the greeting and footer (English, Greek, Italian) and the preview line shown next to the subject. (3) Content: blocks from top to bottom — Heading, Text, Picture, Button, Highlight (title, text, picture, button) and Divider; add a block with the buttons below the list, move it by changing its position number, tick Remove to drop it; **text** prints bold and web addresses become links. The Golden A/S header, the company footer, the reason the person receives the message and the Unsubscribe link are added automatically. (4) Assistant: with the AI assistant switched on for the page Newsletters (Settings › AI assistant), type a brief and press "Write the draft" — it writes subject, preview line and blocks, optionally with recent Marine intelligence headlines; only the brief and public headlines are sent, never contacts or recipients; always read and correct it. (5) Audience: tick the organisation roles (customers, yards, owners, management companies, suppliers, class, consultants, brokers, agents) — every active person with an e-mail address in those organisations is included once; add the website subscribers (Newsletter / Events), a country and extra addresses if needed; the list of recipients can be opened. People who unsubscribed and addresses that bounced are always left out. (6) Preview opens the message as the reader sees it; "Send me a test" sends it to your own address. (7) Send: "Send to n recipients" locks the issue and starts the sending — a few messages with every background run of the portal, up to the daily ceiling; "Send next batch now" pushes it, "Stop sending" cancels what is not sent yet. (8) Statistics on the same page: sent, opened, clicked, unsubscribed, failed / bounced; by group; the links clicked; the organisations that responded; every recipient with the time of opening and clicking. Opens are indicative only (mail programs open or block pictures automatically) — clicks are reliable. When a message comes back to the mailbox, press ↯ on that recipient to mark the address as bounced. (9) Tabs of the list: Website subscribers (pending until they press the confirmation link), Unsubscribed and bounced (an address can be allowed again only when the person asks), Sending settings (mailbox, messages per batch, daily ceiling). Replies arrive in the sending mailbox as normal e-mail.',
      'Who visits the website — networks and organisations (v1.138.1): Commercial › Web Sales › Website statistics has a new card "Networks and organisations". For every visitor of the day the website looks up the name of the connection (reverse DNS), keeps only its registered domain — for example the network of a yard or a management company, or the internet provider of a home or mobile connection — and counts it once, together with the country. The card lists the domains of the chosen period with their visitors; "connection without a name" groups the connections that publish no name. No IP address is stored, on the website or in the portal, and a single person cannot be identified; the website Privacy Policy (R4) describes this. Figures start on the day the website was updated (14 November 2026). Visitors whose browser sends Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control are not counted, as before.',
      'Parts to invoice — delivery notes to accounting (v1.138.0): every line of an issued delivery note to a project or a customer now passes through invoicing, like the man-hours. Open 🧾 Parts to invoice from Procurement › Delivery notes or from Accounting. Four tabs: To decide, To be invoiced, Included in the offer, Invoiced. (1) Procurement (the Procurement right or management) works on "To decide": the lines are grouped per vessel with delivery note, project, part, quantity and unit cost; the unit price is proposed at cost plus the minimum markup and can be changed; choose per line "To be invoiced" or "Included in the offer" — or set all lines at once with the buttons on top — and press Save decisions. Lines left on "—" stay to be decided. The accounting department gets a notification. (2) Accounting works on "To be invoiced": tick the lines that are on one invoice, type the invoice number and date and press "Mark ticked as invoiced"; "Back to procurement" returns lines that should not be invoiced as they are. (3) "Included in the offer" needs no invoice; procurement can still change the decision. (4) "Invoiced" shows number, date and who registered it; for a wrong entry accounting ticks the lines and presses "Undo the invoice", then registers them again. Delivery notes to production (subcontractors) and returns to suppliers are not part of this. The page ⚓ To invoice per vessel shows the same lines of the vessel under "Parts on delivery notes". Note: all lines of delivery notes issued before this version start under "To decide".',
      'Delivery time on offers (v1.137.0): the offer form (Commercial › Offers › an offer › Edit, offers made with the offer builder) has two fields below Validity — "Estimated delivery time — parts" and "Estimated delivery time — labour". Type them as the customer should read them, e.g. "6–8 weeks from order" and "2 weeks after the parts arrive". The PDF and the Word document print a numbered section "Delivery time" with the two lines, after Exclusions and before Validity; a field left empty is not printed, and with both empty there is no such section. If your text templates already have their own delivery wording, put {delivery_parts} and {delivery_labour} in that text block instead — the document then prints your block and no separate section. A firm offer created from a budgetary one keeps the two values.',
      'To invoice per vessel (v1.136.0): on Accounting, click a vessel in "Waiting to be reported" — or the button ⚓ To invoice per vessel and choose one — to see everything that is still to be invoiced for that vessel, not only the hours. Five figures on top and a table for each: (1) Contracts not fully invoiced — every sub-project (or project) with an approved offer, its contract value, what was invoiced and what remains; a red "Final invoice" marks a sub-project or project that is completed, so that invoice closes it; the Invoice button opens a direct invoice for it. (2) Proformas without an accounting invoice — issued proformas not yet covered by an invoice; highlighted when money was already received. (3) Hours not reported yet — approved hours of timesheets and service reports per project and sub-project, with Collect to start the accounting report. (4) Accounting reports not invoiced yet — drafts, returned reports and those waiting at accounting. (5) Materials of service reports — parts marked "I" (to be invoiced) on service reports that are not invoiced yet; 📦 = issued from stock. Amounts are gross as in the ledger and are shown only to people with the finance view or the accounting department. Materials bought through purchase orders for a project are not listed — they are invoiced through the offer of the project.',
      'Correcting an invoice number or date afterwards (v1.135.1): when accounting typed a wrong invoice number or date on an accounting report, open the report — below the detail there is "✎ Correct the invoice number or date" (accounting department and administrators only). It lists every invoice registered on the report with its number, its date and the number of lines; change the number and / or the date, add a reason if you wish, and press Save correction. Only the number and the date change — the lines stay Invoiced, hours and values are untouched; the timesheets show the new number, and the History of the report records "Invoice corrected" with old → new, who and when. An invoice that was issued from the portal (direct invoice) keeps its own number in the ledger — correct that one on the invoice itself.',
      'Two ERP codes per organisation (v1.135.0): the ERP numbers customers and suppliers in two separate ledgers, so an organisation has two fields — "ERP code — customer" and "ERP code — supplier" (Contacts › Companies › edit). The customer code is the one of the billing customer (Settings › Customers shows the same); the supplier code is the one of the supplier card (Procurement › Suppliers shows the same) and is saved only when the organisation has the role Supplier. The two are never copied into each other any more, the supplier import fills only the supplier code, and the same number may exist in both ledgers for different organisations. A supplier code can belong to one organisation only. Contacts › Organisations shows "ERP codes to tidy" while there is something to fix: suppliers that carry their code in the customer field (one button moves them all) and organisations that are customer and supplier with the same code on both sides (open each and type the right codes from the ERP).',
      'Sub-projects of the chosen project only (v1.134.1): on every form with a Project and a Sub-project field — new task, ticket, service and inspection report, function check, offer, production order, direct invoice, requisition, purchase order, planning entry, accounting and external service reports — choosing the project narrows the Sub-project list to the sub-projects of that project. The New task form and the Planning form used to show every sub-project in the search list; this is corrected. On the task form, with no project chosen, the list is grouped under a heading per project, and choosing a sub-project fills in its project. If the list still shows everything after the update, reload the page once (Ctrl+F5).',
      'Profit below the minimum — the red ▼ (v1.134.0): a System Budget compares its profit with the company minimum, 40 % on cost unless management changed it ("Markup on cost": sale = cost × 1,40; this is 28,6 % margin on sale). On the budget page the Totals card shows a red notice when the whole budget is below the minimum, and the Markup on cost of Labour, Parts and the Total turns red with a ▼. Every labour line and part line whose own profit is below the minimum — usually because of a line discount or a lower percentage typed on the line — carries a ▼ next to its sale; point at it to read the figures. When the total is fine but some lines are not, a grey notice says how many. Travelling expenses and other costs are not marked (they are normally passed on at cost), and excluded parts are skipped. In the list of budgets a ▼ stands next to the sale of a budget below the minimum. The preview before "Convert to offer" shows the Markup on cost of the offer in red with a notice when it is below the minimum (it counts the commission when the offer includes it). The mark is a warning only — nothing is blocked. Administrators and management set the percentage at the top of Work › System Budgets ("Minimum profit on cost %"; 0 switches the mark off).',
      'Apps showing "Sync error" (v1.133.3): between 10 and 12 November 2026 the phone apps (Service Engineer, Golden PM, Golden HR and the customer app) could not reach the portal and showed "Sync error". This is repaired on the server — no app update is needed. Open the app and pull to refresh, or wait for the next automatic sync: reports, hours, photos and requests that were saved on the phone in the meantime are still in its queue and are sent now. If an app still shows the error, sign out and in again.',
      'Project buttons with all sub-projects completed (v1.133.2): on Work › Projects a project whose sub-projects are all completed shows "n completed hidden · Show completed" and, below it, the usual buttons — Edit project, Add sub-project, Drawings, Weekly and Monthly report, Offers, Job Orders, Service Reports and (administrators) Delete. Before, the buttons appeared only after "Show completed".',
      'System Budgets grouped per vessel (v1.133.1): Work › System Budgets lists the budgets under a heading per vessel — the vessel of the budget\'s project — with the number of budgets, the hours and the sale of that vessel; the vessel name opens the vessel page. The vessels follow the order of their most urgent budget, so the vessel with the most pressing request is on top, and inside a vessel the budgets keep the order priority › date the offer must be ready. Budgets that have no project yet come last under "Without a vessel". The filter "Group by" switches between Vessel and No grouping (the flat list, which can be sorted by any column), and the search box also finds a vessel name.',
      'Web Sales (v1.133.0): the Website inbox and the Website statistics moved from Work to Commercial › Web Sales — one menu entry, one page with three tabs: Website inbox (the number beside it is the count of new inquiries), Website statistics and Marine intelligence. Links in notifications open the right tab as before.',
      'Marine intelligence (v1.133.0): Commercial › Web Sales › Marine intelligence shows the headlines of the week in five groups — Newbuilding yachts (Europe and Middle East), Refit news (Europe), Energy storage systems for marine, Hybrid vessels (marine technology) and Yachting technology news. Each line gives the headline, the source and the date; a click opens the article on the publisher\'s site in a new tab. "Older week" and "Newer week" move through the last six months. The portal reads its news sources by itself, one at a time through the day; "Read the sources now" reads them all at once. The grouping is done by the words of the headline, so now and then a headline lands in the wrong group or is not relevant: management and sales can Move it to another group or Hide it, and can add a headline by hand (title, link, group, short note).',
      'Yard, flag and class in the vessel list (v1.132.1): Work › Vessels shows Yard, Flag and Class as three columns (a dash where the field is empty), and the search box also finds a flag or a class — type "Malta" or "RINA" to list those vessels.',
      'Class and flag of a vessel (v1.132.0): the vessel form has the fields Class (classification society — a list suggests the usual ones, any text is accepted) and Flag, beside IMO and Yard. They show under the vessel name on its page, under the yard in the vessel list, and in the Excel export.',
      'E-mail card — draft in your Outlook (v1.131.1): the button "Draft in my Outlook" places the message in the Drafts folder of your own Microsoft 365 mailbox — in HTML format, with the recipients, Cc, subject, text and the ticked attachments (up to 25 MB). Open Outlook › Drafts — the new Outlook, Outlook on the web and the phone all show it within a few seconds — change what you like and press Send; it is sent from your address like any message you write yourself. The portal also shows a button that opens the draft in Outlook on the web. Use it when you want to read or edit the message before it leaves; use "Send now" when it can go as it is. The file "Draft file for classic Outlook (.eml)" stays for the classic Outlook only. Administrators: this button needs the application permission Mail.ReadWrite (with admin consent) on the portal mailer in Microsoft Entra; without it the portal answers that Microsoft 365 did not allow the draft.',
      'E-mail card — send from your own mailbox (v1.131.0): the ✉ E-mail card of an offer, a report, a proforma, a purchase order, a statement or a candidate has three buttons. "Send now from my mailbox" (gold) sends the message at once through Microsoft 365 from your own address, in HTML format with the ticked attachments; it is kept in the Sent Items of your Outlook and replies come to you — nothing has to be opened or downloaded. The portal asks for confirmation first, because the message leaves immediately; check recipients, subject and text before. Limits: attachments up to 2,9 MB together, 30 recipients, 40 messages per hour — above that use the draft. "Outlook draft (.eml)" downloads the message for classic Outlook, now in HTML format, to edit before sending (the new Outlook and Outlook on the web cannot edit such a file). "Mail app" opens the default mail program with recipients and text only. "Send now" appears when the portal sends through Microsoft Graph and your portal e-mail address is a company mailbox; administrators switch it on or off, and see every message sent this way, in Settings › System › Website.',
      'Markup on cost and margin on sale in a System Budget (v1.130.1): the percentage typed for parts — in the header ("Markup on parts") and on a part line ("Markup on cost %") — is added to the cost: sale = cost + that percentage of the cost. The Totals card shows the profit of each group in two columns: "Markup on cost" = (sale − cost) ÷ cost, the figure you typed, and "Margin on sale" = (sale − cost) ÷ sale. They are two readings of the same profit: 50 % on cost is 33,3 % on sale, 40 % on cost is 28,6 % on sale; to keep half of the sale price as profit the markup on cost must be 100 %. Only the names changed — no price of any budget changed.',
      'System Budget from a sub-project (v1.130.0): open the sub-project — the card "System Budgets" (under the offers) lists the budgets of that sub-project with number, priority, status, the date the offer must be ready, owner, who asked and the sale. To start the process fill only two fields: Priority and "Offer ready by" (optional, not in the past), then "Request a System Budget". The budget is created under the sub-project and its project, as a draft, with the title [PROJECT]-[SUB-PROJECT]-[Description] (for example GY-85-02-Y.01-Bridge); nothing else has to be typed. It appears at once in Work › System Budgets, where the list is ordered by priority and an overdue date shows in red — the technical department works from there. The project manager of the project gets a notification. The person who asks is the first owner; the owner can be changed in the budget header. If the sub-project already has an open budget the portal asks before creating another. Shown to the people who may use System Budgets (management, project managers, sales).',
      'Travelling expenses at cost plus a percentage (v1.129.0): the table "Travelling expenses and other costs" of a System Budget has the column "On cost %". Type the cost and a percentage on a line — the Sale becomes cost + that percentage (10 on a cost of 400,00 gives 440,00; 0 means at cost) and a sale typed on the same line is replaced. Leave the percentage empty to type the sale yourself, as before. The percentage is kept in revisions, copies, templates and the Excel export.',
      'Discount per line in a System Budget (v1.129.0): the Labour table and the Parts table have the column "Disc. %". Type a percentage (0–100) on a line and save: the Sale of that line is lowered by it — for a labour line the whole line, hours and daily allowance; for a part line quantity × unit sale. The cost does not change, so the margin of the budget shows what the discount costs. The undiscounted amount stays visible, struck through, under the sale; the total row of each table and the Totals card show the sum of "Line discounts given". An empty field is no discount. The discount is kept in revisions, copies, templates and the Excel export. At the conversion to an offer with "one line per line" each offer line carries its discount %, so the customer sees price, discount and net amount; the daily allowance appears as one line per discount percentage. With grouped labour (per section, one lump sum) or parts as a lump sum the offer line carries the already discounted amount and shows no percentage.',
      'Rate cards per zone in a System Budget (v1.128.3): the Rate card list of a labour line shows the hourly rate of the budget\'s zone — with the zone Outside Attica / Abroad / Greek islands it shows and applies the out-of-Attica rate of the rate list (before, a rate list with separate rows for the two zones gave the local rate for both). A budget that was priced with the wrong rate shows the yellow note on the labour card; "Apply the rate-card rates" corrects its lines.',
      'Rate zone of a System Budget (v1.128.2): the header field "Rate zone" has two values, the two price columns of the rate list — Local (Attica) and Outside Attica / Abroad / Greek islands (one column covers all three). Changing the zone and saving the header now re-prices the labour lines that carry a rate card: a line that had the card\'s rate of the old zone takes the rate of the new zone, and the totals, the offer preview and the conversion follow. A rate that was typed by hand (or came from an imported workbook) is kept; the labour card then shows a yellow note with the number of such lines and the button "Apply the rate-card rates", which sets every rate-card line to the rate of the zone. Lines without a rate card (flat-rate or free lines) are not touched — give them a rate card or change the rate by hand. A finished budget must be reopened first.',
      'Preview of a vessel\'s web link (v1.128.1): when a vessel has a web link, its page shows a preview card under the name — the site, the title of the linked page and its short description (usually the vessel\'s particulars) — with the button "Open the page", which opens the page in a new tab. In Work › Vessels a 🌐 icon beside the vessel name opens the same page directly. Nobody has to open Edit to reach the link. The portal reads the title and the description when the link is saved; ↻ on the card reads them again. A page that cannot be read (it needs a sign-in, or it blocks automatic readers) shows the site name and the button only. Shown to Golden A/S staff only.',
      'Web link of a vessel (v1.128.0): Work › Vessels › Edit has the field "Online information (web link)" — the address of a place where information about the vessel is kept online (a shared folder, the yard\'s or the manager\'s page, a register entry). The vessel\'s page then shows the button "Online information" with the name of the site; it opens in a new tab. Only an address starting with http:// or https:// is accepted (www… is completed with https://). The button is shown to Golden A/S staff only — customers do not see it.',
      'Signed out when you come back (v1.127.7): the portal signs a person out when its last tab has been closed for more than about 45 seconds ("the browser was closed" — Settings › Login history › Sign out when the browser is closed; management can switch it off). Opening the portal after that leads straight to the sign-in page with that message — the dashboard no longer appears for a moment first. Going from the portal to the entry page goldensas.com and back in the same browser keeps you signed in while that page stays open.',
      'Who is online: the green counter on the topbar opens the list of colleagues connected now with their time online; My Account › Who is online shows the sign-in time, the uptime and the last activity. Sign in with your company e-mail and password; the code of the authenticator app follows (two-factor sign-in). The first time: My Account › set your photo (it appears next to your name everywhere), your language (English, Greek, Italian, Bulgarian, Ukrainian), the theme (light / dark) and your world clocks — up to seven places for the clock strip at the top of every page (UTC is always first; Back to the company set restores the default). Passkeys (My Account › Passkeys): add this device once — your phone or PC asks for Face ID / fingerprint / PIN — and from then on the login page offers "Sign in with a passkey" (your browser may suggest it in the e-mail field), replacing password and authenticator code; a passkey is one device (or your Apple / Google keychain) bound to your account, nothing biometric leaves the device; remove it on the same page if the device is lost. The same passkey signs you in to the mobile apps (login screen › Sign in with Face ID / passkey) when the app runs in the browser or installed from the browser. 🔔 Notifications at the top is where every decision, wish and reminder arrives.',
      'The left menu — what matters for you: Dashboard (your attention list, celebrations, fleet & activity, your program of the week), HR › My leave (balances, requests, periods, trainings, evaluations, messages with HR), Work › Planning (where you are planned, day by day), Community 🤝, Support › Manual and Knowledge base. Accounts scoped "Employee" see only My program, My leave, the manual, notifications and the account. On a phone the same My leave page is in the Golden A/S Service app under More › My HR (install from goldensas.com/customer/mobile/).',
      'Leaves allowed by law (v1.86.5): My leave, the Service app\'s My HR and the HR app no longer list parental, paternity / maternity, marriage, carer, force majeure, child illness, study, school hours, sick and L.104 leave among the entitlements. They sit in their own block "Allowed by law — on request" with the ceiling the law provides ("up to 5", "per event") and what was taken — never an "available" figure — with a note that they are not an entitlement to be used up but what the law allows when the event occurs, on request and with the supporting document. Annual leave, paid hours and early-leave hours remain entitlements.',
      'Reason and medical certificate (v1.87.0): every leave request needs a reason chosen from a list that follows the leave type (vacation, personal, family, bereavement, wedding, exams, public duty, force majeure … for leave; illness, accident, medical examination, surgery, hospitalisation for sick; birth, child care, child illness, school, prenatal for parental); "Other" needs a description. The manager may correct the reason when approving (the decision row has the reason selector) and HR may correct it at any time (✎ Correct reason under the request). A request for a medical reason may be sent without the doctor\'s certificate: it gets a due date 5 working days after the first day of absence, shown as a gold "certificate due by" pill (red when overdue) with an Upload button — on My leave, in the Service app and for HR under the request. The employee is reminded the day before, employee and HR when it is overdue; the HR dashboard lists the certificates still due. Works the same in the Service app (0.6.15) and the Golden HR app (1.0.3).',
      'Missing supporting documents (v1.87.1): My leave opens with a notice listing the requests that still lack their document — a medical certificate with its due date (red when overdue) or a type that requires a document filed without one — each with its upload button below; the Service app shows the same notice on My HR and a red badge on the home card; on the HR side the person\'s card carries a "📄 n supporting document(s) missing" pill on the Requests tab and the HR dashboard lists the medical certificates due. Leave reasons are translated in all portal languages (EN, EL, IT, BG, UK) on the web and in the apps.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'balance', 'title' => 'My leave — balances', 'body' => [
      'HR › My leave shows for the year, per leave type: entitled (the statutory annual leave is computed by the portal from your service — 20 to 26 days, pro rata in the year of hiring), carried over from last year, taken, pending (requested, not yet decided) and available; red means over the entitlement. Hour types accumulate in hours: school visits have their own allowance (4 days per child a year, in hours); early leaves / late arrivals are a company permission — the hours add up and every completed 8 hours takes one day of your annual leave; what is left is shown under your annual balance ("4 h accumulated · 4 h to the next day"). If a figure looks wrong, write to HR from the same page (Messages with HR) — HR can see the computation and correct it.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'request', 'title' => 'Requesting leave', 'body' => [
      ['ol' => ['My leave › New request: choose the type (annual leave, sick, paternity / maternity, marriage, child care, school visit, strike, unpaid …), the dates (or hours for hour types) and the reason when the type asks for it; attach the document when the type requires one (medical certificate …). The portal counts working days only — weekends and public holidays are never charged.', 'Send. Your manager decides first (types marked HR only, such as sick leave, go straight to HR), then HR. You are notified at every step; a note from the approver appears with the decision.', 'Approved leave appears on the Planning board (one chip per day) and moves from pending to taken; a rejected request returns the days to your balance.', 'While a request is pending you can cancel it yourself; afterwards ask HR to correct it.']],
      ['fig' => 'flow-timesheet.svg', 'cap' => 'The approval ladder — you, your manager, HR, then the board'],
      'Maternity and paternity leave are once per birth: HR keeps your children\'s birth years on your card; days already taken at a previous employer for the same birth are deducted — tell HR.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'periods', 'title' => 'Leave periods, groups and company closures', 'body' => [
      'HR publishes leave periods (the summer groups, a quiet week in a yard) on My leave › Open periods. Join one with your own dates inside the period — your manager approves and the leave is recorded without a second request. Periods marked 🔒 Mandatory are company closures (Christmas week, August): HR assigns them to everybody, you cannot join or opt out; the working days of the closure are charged to your annual leave automatically. "Full" means the places of a period are taken.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'program', 'title' => 'My program — the Planning board', 'body' => [
      'System Budgets (v1.123.0): the Work › System Budgets menu is shown to Sales, project managers and management only. If you need to see a budget, ask your project manager.',
      'Engineer on service-report time lines (v1.116.1): write the engineer who actually did the hours on every line — accounting bills by that name. The first line is pre-filled with your own name only if you are technical staff; when you prepare a report for colleagues the field starts empty and the list offers the technical staff.',
      'Specialty on service-report time lines (v1.114.1): pick the technical discipline the hours belong to — Electrical, Automation, Navigation & Communication, Service, Engineering, Production, Project Management — each shown with its rate card (e.g. "Automation · card D — Automation engineer") — plus the specialist cards of the rate list (Atlas engineers, Magnus, Aventics …) for hours billed at those tariffs; it is pre-filled with your own discipline. Accounting bills those hours at that discipline\'s tariff, so choose the discipline of the work actually done, not your job title.',
      'Completed sub-projects (v1.114.0): a sub-project the PM set to Completed no longer appears in the sub-project lists of your forms (timesheets, service and inspection reports, tasks, tickets, drawings, risks) nor in the Projects tree — the tree says "n completed hidden ›" to show them. A report or timesheet that already points to a completed sub-project keeps it.',
      'Work › Planning (or My program for employee accounts) shows where you are planned day by day: sub-projects and vessels in colour, your leave, the days off of your calendar. Click a chip to see the sub-project, the open items and the contacts. The week view on the dashboard shows the next seven days. Changes are made by the project managers and HR — if your program is wrong, tell your PM.',
      ['fig' => 'g-colour-code.jpg', 'cap' => 'The colour code of the chips'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'training', 'title' => 'Training opportunities, my trainings and certificates', 'body' => [
      ['ol' => ['My leave › Training opportunities lists the trainings you are invited to — introductory, internal or external — with dates, hours, places and the apply-by date. Open the programme, add a note if you like and press Apply. Your manager or HR approves; you are notified.', 'My trainings keeps your record: status (applied, approved, attended), result and certificate — number, valid-until date and the scanned copy to download. Expiring certificates are followed by HR; the refresher is published as a new training.', 'Introductory trainings are part of the onboarding of a new colleague; internal ones are given by Golden A/S engineers — propose topics to HR through Messages with HR.']],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'evaluation', 'title' => 'My annual evaluation', 'body' => [
      'My leave › How we evaluate you explains the whole method and shows your computed scores so far for the running year — read it at the start of the year, not at the end. Once a year your manager (with HR) writes your evaluation: eight criteria scored 1 to 5 — quality of work, productivity, technical skills, teamwork, initiative, reliability, safety & procedures, communication — with strengths, areas to improve, goals for next year and training needs; two more scores are computed by the portal from the year\'s facts — training participation (what you attended, ranked among colleagues) and attendance & consistency (early leaves / late arrivals, unplanned absences, leave asked at the last moment) — and the targets of the year are summed up. When it is final you are notified; read it on My leave › My evaluations, add your own comment if you wish, and press Acknowledge. Acknowledging means you have read it, not necessarily that you agree — write your view in the comment; it stays with the evaluation. Your evaluations of past years stay on the same page.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'utilization', 'title' => 'Your utilization (v1.98.0)', 'body' => [
      'HR and your manager see, per month and per year, how your working time was used: hours on projects from your timesheets, internal work and absences from the planning board, and the rest as idle hours — days without a project or work. A day you worked but did not book on a timesheet counts as idle, so book every day on the week\'s timesheet and submit it; planned days without a timesheet are flagged. Your own report is under HR › Utilization when the page is open to you.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'targets', 'title' => 'My targets', 'body' => [
      'My leave › My targets lists the targets of the year that concern you — the company\'s, your department\'s and your own — with how each is measured, the target value, the due date, the progress and the status. Update the progress of your own targets whenever something moves (a note helps your manager); your manager closes them as achieved or missed at year end. New targets reach you as a notification. Their achievement is part of your annual evaluation.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'messages', 'title' => 'Messages with HR and your personal data', 'body' => [
      'My leave › Messages with HR is a private thread between you and HR — nobody else, not even your manager, can read it. Use it for balances, certificates, documents, personal data changes (address, phone, bank account, family status — HR keeps them on your card; you can see your own data on My leave). HR is notified of every message and you of every reply.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'community', 'title' => 'Community — wishes, kudos, noticeboard, badges', 'body' => [
      'Celebrations on the dashboard: the birthdays, name days, work anniversaries and new colleagues of the next 14 days. On the day (and the day before) a Send wishes button appears — pick an emoji, add a few words, send; the colleague is notified and sees everybody\'s wishes on his own dashboard. Every morning the portal posts the celebrations of the day in Notifications.',
      'Kudos: thank a colleague for a job well done — from the dashboard or Community, choose the person and say what for in one line (up to three a day to the same person). The kudos wall and the most-thanked lists are on Community.',
      'Noticeboard (Community): company news, polls and photos. React (👍 ❤️ 👏 🎉 😂 🚢), comment — type @ to mention a colleague, who is notified — vote; share a photo of the month from the field, or attach a photo to any post or poll (jpg / png / webp up to 12 MB). Badges are earned automatically from your work in the portal (timesheets, tickets done, reports, Q&A answers, trainings attended, kudos …) and show next to your name. The winner of the month is named from the activity ranking of the dashboard — the score counts what you create in the portal: tickets, comments, timesheets, reports, answers, requests.',
      'New colleague: your onboarding checklist and your buddy are on your dashboard until all eight items are done — photo, manual, personal data checked, first timesheet, found on the Planning board, leave balance checked, introductory training, coffee with the buddy.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'card', 'title' => 'Completing my employee card with the HR Assistant', 'body' => [
      'My leave shows how complete your employee card is. "Complete my card with the HR Assistant" opens a guided flow in six short steps: identity and family, contact and address, identification numbers and bank, education and work, documents, review and submit. Every field is pre-filled with what HR holds today — confirm, correct or leave it. You can stop at any time and continue later; the same flow is reachable from HR › HR Assistant.',
      ['ul' => [
        'Nothing is written to your card before HR approves. Your identification numbers (AMKA, ΑΦΜ, ID, passport, IBAN) are encrypted while the flow is open and only HR can read them.',
        'The first step shows how your data is handled (who is responsible, why it is kept, who sees it, how long, your rights); tick "I have read the notice" to begin.',
        'The portal checks what you type: the AMKA must be 11 digits starting with a date (normally your date of birth — if yours was issued with another date, tick "My AMKA was issued with this date" and HR will see the note), the ΑΦΜ check digit and the IBAN must be valid. If your account has two-factor authentication, the identification step and the submission ask for the 6-digit code.',
        '✦ Let the assistant fill it in: describe your details in your own words, or photograph your ID card or passport. The assistant proposes values — press Use on the ones that are right; the photo is only read, not stored. Attach the scan itself in the Documents step (PDF, JPG, PNG, WebP).',
        'Review & submit lists only what you changed, next to what the card holds; tick the declaration and submit. HR may return the flow with a note (you will find it on top of the flow), reject it, or approve it — you are notified each time, and the approved values appear on your card.',
        'Withdraw cancels an open flow; the proposed values are discarded.',
      ]],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'requisitions', 'title' => 'Asking for parts — purchase requisitions (v1.91.0)', 'body' => [
      'If your discipline is enabled for requisitions (or you are a PM), the left menu shows Procurement › Requisitions. "+ New requisition": pick the project and sub-project, the purpose (project, stock, service call) and the date you need the parts by; create it, then add the lines — search the inventory by code or description (the picker shows the free stock) or type a free-text line with maker and part number. "Submit to Procurement" freezes the lines; Procurement checks the parts budget of the sub-project and approves (stock reserved, the rest bought), sends it to management if it is over budget, or rejects with a reason — you are notified at every step and can follow the log on the request. The warehouse issues the reserved parts to you; the request closes when every line is issued.',
      'Engineering BOM (v1.92.0): if you are in the team of a sub-project (or your discipline is enabled), the Procurement tab of the sub-project lets you keep its bill of materials as revisions — upload the file or type the lines, see what the stock covers, issue the revision to Procurement, and answer their change proposals (accept or reject with a reason). The approved revision is the current BOM; one click makes a requisition for the lines the stock does not cover.',
      'BOM import (v1.113.0): use the template from the BOM page; after the upload review each row — the portal proposes the inventory item (by Golden code, maker part number or the assistant) with its stock; Accept, Reject (free text), pick another item or create a New inventory item, then Apply.',
      'Goods receipt (v1.94.0, Warehouse flag): on a sent purchase order the Goods receipt box lets you book what arrived per line — received and rejected quantities with the reason (damaged, wrong type, missing) and the action (return, replacement, credit), delivery note and a scan. Accepted parts go into the warehouse at the order price and are reserved for the requisition they were bought for; the requester is told and you issue them from the requisition as usual.',
      'Item documents and software licences (v1.105.0): on an inventory item card you find the links Procurement added — datasheet, manual, drawing, certificate, software download, supplier page. Software items show their licences (product, version, seats, expiry, where assigned); the licence key itself is visible only to procurement administrators. When a delivery note was issued to your project, the goods are already out of the warehouse — the note (number, PDF) is linked on the purchase order receipt and listed under Inventory › Delivery notes.',
      'Parts from the phone (v1.97.0 — Service app 0.6.18, Golden HR 1.0.4; v1.103.1: tap the item box to see the recently used items): Home › 🧾 Parts & requisitions lists your requisitions (Open / Mine / All) and "+ New requisition" raises one from the phone — project, sub-project, purpose, needed-by, title, then lines: search the inventory by code or description, scan the barcode with 📷, or type a free-text part with maker and part number, set the quantity and unit, "+ Add line", and Submit to Procurement. Open a request to follow it: state, every line with reserved · ordered · received · issued, the log of decisions, and Cancel while nothing has been reserved. You need to be online; the same request appears in the browser under Procurement › Requisitions.',
      'Hours not chargeable (v1.119.0): when hours on your service report must not be charged to the customer, set the line to NC and choose why — Warranty, Goodwill, Internal / own work, Training, Rework or Other (portal form and Service app 0.6.21). You decide it once, on the report; accounting only confirms it.',
      'Parts on service reports (v1.96.0): next to each spare part of a task type the inventory code (or scan the barcode with the 📷 button in the Service app 0.6.17) and the quantity; when you save the report the part is taken off the stock automatically and costed to the job, and a 📦 mark appears. Parts that are not in the inventory stay as plain text.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'help', 'title' => 'Help and good habits', 'body' => [
      ['ul' => ['Support › Knowledge base: ask a question, answer a colleague\'s — answers earn a badge.', 'Support › Portal requests: report a bug or propose an improvement; the developer answers with a decision and the release that carries it.', 'Keep your leave requests ahead of time — approved leave is what the PMs plan around.', 'Check Notifications once a day; the Monday digest sums up the week (celebrations, open trainings, new posts, last week\'s top three).', 'Your password and two-factor device are yours alone — never share them; sign out on shared computers.']],
